__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,_e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(_e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),_e.useAsyncStorage=function(e){return{getItem:(...t)=>u.default.getItem(e,...t),setItem:(...t)=>u.default.setItem(e,...t),mergeItem:(...t)=>u.default.mergeItem(e,...t),removeItem:(...t)=>u.default.removeItem(e,...t)}};var e,t=r(d[0]),u=(e=t)&&e.__esModule?e:{default:e}},854,[850]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"atheismTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'atheism',category:'atheism',title:'Talking with Atheists',question:'How should I engage a friend who says there\'s no evidence for God?',shortAnswer:'Listen first, find out why they don\'t believe, and remember that "no evidence" usually means "no evidence I find convincing." Then share the cumulative case and, most of all, your honest reasons for trusting Christ.',overview:['Atheists come to their views for many reasons: intellectual objections, bad experiences with religion, personal suffering, or simply never having considered the evidence. Knowing which one applies changes the conversation.','Apologetics works best as a cumulative case. No single argument has to carry all the weight; like strands of a rope, they gain strength together.'],points:[{heading:'Ask about their story',body:'"Were you ever religious?" "What would change your mind?" These questions build relationship and reveal whether the obstacle is intellectual, emotional or moral.'},{heading:'Share the burden of proof',body:'Some atheists define atheism as merely "lacking belief." But anyone who claims "there is no God" or "naturalism is true" is making a claim that needs support too.'},{heading:'Build a cumulative case',body:'The beginning of the universe, fine-tuning, objective morality, consciousness and the historical evidence for the resurrection all point in the same direction. Together they form a strong case.'},{heading:'Point to Jesus',body:'Ultimately the question is not just whether some god exists but who Jesus is. Invite your friend to read one of the Gospels (Mark or John is a good start) with an open mind.'}],objections:[{objection:'Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence.',response:'Every claim requires sufficient evidence. The existence of a finely tuned universe that began to exist is itself extraordinary, and the claim that it arose from nothing, for no reason, is the more extraordinary one.'},{objection:'You\'re an atheist about every god except one; I just go one god further.',response:'Rejecting specific gods for good reasons is different from rejecting God altogether. The question is whether there is a Creator at all, not which culture\'s deities to discard. The arguments for a transcendent Creator do not apply to Zeus or Thor, which are beings within the universe.'}],verses:[{ref:'Psalm 14:1',quote:!0},{ref:'Jeremiah 29:13',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 17:22-31'}],searchTerms:'answering atheist arguments',keyPassage:'Acts 17',furtherReading:['Gunning for God by John Lennox','The Reason for God by Timothy Keller','I Don\'t Have Enough Faith to Be an Atheist by Geisler and Turek']},{id:'miracles',category:'atheism',title:'Are Miracles Possible?',question:'Doesn\'t science show that miracles can\'t happen?',shortAnswer:'Science describes how nature normally works; it cannot rule out that God, who made nature, might act in it. If God exists, miracles are possible, and each claim should be judged on its evidence.',overview:['The most influential objection comes from the philosopher David Hume (1748), who argued that a miracle violates the laws of nature, which are established by "uniform experience," so testimony to a miracle can never be believed.','Many philosophers, including non-Christians, now regard Hume\'s argument as flawed.'],points:[{heading:'The real question is whether God exists',body:'If there is no God, miracles are impossible. If there is a God who created the universe, then a miracle is no harder for him than creation. The evidence for God\'s existence makes miracles live options.'},{heading:'Hume\'s argument is circular',body:'Saying "uniform experience" is against miracles assumes that no one has ever experienced one, which is the very question at issue. The philosopher of science John Earman, himself not a Christian, titled his critique Hume\'s Abject Failure.'},{heading:'Laws describe, they don\'t forbid',body:'C.S. Lewis noted that if you put $10 in a drawer and then another $10, arithmetic says you\'ll find $20. If a thief takes some, the laws of arithmetic haven\'t been broken; an outside agent intervened. Likewise, natural laws tell us what happens when nothing outside nature acts.'},{heading:'Weigh the evidence case by case',body:'We should be skeptical of many miracle claims. But openness to evidence means not ruling out the resurrection in advance. Scholar Craig Keener has documented a large number of well-attested modern miracle claims, many from eyewitnesses.'}],objections:[{objection:'Ancient people were gullible and believed in miracles easily.',response:'Ancient people knew perfectly well that virgins don\'t conceive and the dead stay dead. That is why Joseph planned to end his engagement (Matthew 1:19) and why Thomas refused to believe until he saw the risen Jesus (John 20:24-27).'},{objection:'Miracles would make science impossible.',response:'Miracles are, by definition, rare exceptions against a background of regular natural law. The founders of modern science believed in both, and saw no conflict: regular laws are what make a miracle recognizable as a sign.'}],verses:[{ref:'Luke 1:37',quote:!0},{ref:'John 20:30-31',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 1:18-19'},{ref:'Acts 2:22'}],searchTerms:'are miracles possible David Hume',keyPassage:'John 20',furtherReading:['Miracles by C.S. Lewis','Miracles by Craig Keener','In Defense of Miracles, ed. Geivett and Habermas']},{id:'scientism',category:'atheism',title:'Is Science the Only Way to Know?',question:'"I only believe what science can prove." Is that a reasonable position?',shortAnswer:'The claim that only science gives knowledge cannot itself be proven by science, so it refutes itself. Science is a wonderful tool, but we know many things, in logic, history, ethics and personal relationships, that science cannot test.',overview:['Scientism is the view that science is the only reliable source of knowledge. It is common in popular culture, though many scientists and philosophers reject it.','Christians love science. The issue is not science but the philosophical claim that nothing outside it can be known.'],points:[{heading:'It saws off the branch it sits on',body:'No experiment can show that "only scientific claims are knowable." It is a philosophical claim, and by its own standard it should be rejected.'},{heading:'Science depends on things it can\'t prove',body:'Science assumes that the world is orderly, that our senses and reasoning are generally reliable, that logic and mathematics hold, and that honesty in reporting data matters. These are presuppositions of science, not results of it.'},{heading:'We know much more than science can test',body:'Historical events can\'t be repeated in a lab, yet we know Julius Caesar was assassinated. We know that cruelty is wrong, that 2 + 2 = 4, and that we are loved by people close to us. None of these is known by the scientific method.'},{heading:'Some questions are beyond science',body:'Science can tell us how the universe works, but not why there is a universe at all, why it is intelligible, or what our lives are for. These are the questions religion and philosophy address.'}],objections:[{objection:'Religion is just a "God of the gaps" that shrinks as science advances.',response:'The best arguments for God are based on what we know, not on what we don\'t: the universe\'s beginning, its fine-tuning, its intelligibility. Newton didn\'t believe in God because he couldn\'t explain gravity; he saw his explanation of gravity as uncovering God\'s handiwork.'},{objection:'Faith is believing without evidence, and science is believing with evidence.',response:'That is not the biblical meaning of faith. See the topic "Faith and Evidence."'}],verses:[{ref:'Proverbs 1:7',quote:!0},{ref:'Colossians 2:8',quote:!0},{ref:'Psalm 19:1',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'scientism is science the only way to know truth',keyPassage:'Proverbs 1',furtherReading:['God\'s Undertaker by John Lennox','Scientism and Secularism by J.P. Moreland']},{id:'mind-and-reason',category:'atheism',title:'Can Matter Explain the Mind?',question:'If we are just products of evolution, can we trust our own reasoning?',shortAnswer:'If our minds were produced only by unguided processes aimed at survival rather than truth, we would have reason to doubt our beliefs, including the belief in naturalism. Theism explains why reason and consciousness exist and can be trusted.',overview:['Consciousness, the inner experience of being you, is one of the deepest puzzles in philosophy. Philosopher David Chalmers called it "the hard problem": why does physical brain activity feel like anything at all?','Even some atheist philosophers, such as Thomas Nagel in Mind and Cosmos (2012), argue that materialism cannot account for mind, reason and value.'],points:[{heading:'The argument from reason',body:'If every thought is fully determined by blind physical causes, then our beliefs are held because of chemistry, not because they are true. C.S. Lewis and later Alvin Plantinga argued that naturalism undermines confidence in the very reasoning used to argue for it.'},{heading:'Darwin\'s "horrid doubt"',body:'In an 1881 letter, Charles Darwin wrote of his "horrid doubt" whether the convictions of a mind developed from lower animals are of any value or trustworthy. The worry is not new.'},{heading:'Consciousness doesn\'t fit materialism',body:'We can describe every neuron firing and still not have explained the experience of seeing red or feeling joy. Christianity says reality is fundamentally personal: God is mind, and he made us to share in knowing.'},{heading:'Made in the image of a rational God',body:'If we are made in the image of a rational Creator (Genesis 1:27), we would expect our minds to be capable of grasping truth, including the mathematical order of the universe.'}],objections:[{objection:'Neuroscience shows that the mind is just the brain.',response:'Neuroscience shows that mind and brain are closely connected: damage one and you affect the other. Connection is not identity. A pianist needs a piano, but the music isn\'t the same thing as the instrument.'},{objection:'Evolution favors true beliefs, because true beliefs help us survive.',response:'That may be true for practical beliefs, like "that lion is dangerous." But natural selection rewards behavior, not truth, and has no obvious stake in abstract beliefs about philosophy or metaphysics, including naturalism itself.'}],verses:[{ref:'Genesis 1:27',quote:!0},{ref:'Psalm 139:14',quote:!0},{ref:'Isaiah 1:18',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'argument from reason consciousness naturalism',keyPassage:'Genesis 1',furtherReading:['Where the Conflict Really Lies by Alvin Plantinga','Miracles by C.S. Lewis (chapter 3)','Mind and Cosmos by Thomas Nagel (an atheist\'s critique)']},{id:'religion-and-violence',category:'atheism',title:'Hasn\'t Religion Caused Most Wars?',question:'Wouldn\'t the world be more peaceful without religion?',shortAnswer:'Most wars have been fought over land, power and politics. The twentieth century\'s explicitly atheist regimes killed tens of millions. And violence done in Jesus\' name contradicts his teaching, while violence done in the name of atheism does not contradict anything.',overview:['Crusades, inquisitions and religious wars are real and shameful parts of history, and Christians should not excuse them. But the claim that religion is the main cause of war does not match the evidence.','A better question is whether a movement\'s violence follows from its founder\'s teaching or betrays it.'],points:[{heading:'Most wars are not religious',body:'Historians who have catalogued wars find that only a small minority, by one widely cited count under ten percent, were primarily religious. Most were driven by territory, resources, power and nationalism.'},{heading:'Secular regimes and mass killing',body:'The Soviet Union under Stalin, China under Mao and Cambodia under the Khmer Rouge were officially atheist and responsible for tens of millions of deaths in the twentieth century. Removing religion does not remove human evil.'},{heading:'Measure a movement by its founder',body:'Jesus taught his followers to love their enemies (Matthew 5:44), told Peter to put away his sword (Matthew 26:52), and said his kingdom is not advanced by fighting (John 18:36). Christians who used violence acted against Jesus, not in line with him.'},{heading:'The fruit of the gospel',body:'Christian faith motivated the founding of hospitals and universities, the abolition of slavery by people like William Wilberforce, and countless works of mercy that continue today.'}],objections:[{objection:'What about the Crusades and the Inquisition?',response:'They involved real wrongs that Christians should acknowledge. Historians also note they are often misunderstood and exaggerated: the Crusades were in part a response to centuries of conquest, and historians such as Henry Kamen estimate the Spanish Inquisition executed a few thousand people over three centuries, not millions. Christians condemn them by Jesus\' own standard.'},{objection:'Religious certainty makes people dangerous.',response:'Certainty is dangerous when its content is dangerous. Being certain that you should love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you produces martyrs, not murderers.'}],verses:[{ref:'Matthew 5:44',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 26:52',quote:!0},{ref:'John 18:36',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'religion causes war violence',keyPassage:'Matthew 5',furtherReading:['The Myth of Religious Violence by William Cavanaugh','Atheist Delusions by David Bentley Hart','Dominion by Tom Holland']}]},855,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"bibleTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'manuscripts',category:'bible',title:'Can We Trust the Manuscripts?',question:'Hasn\'t the Bible been changed and copied so many times we can\'t know what it originally said?',shortAnswer:'No ancient book is better attested. We have over 5,800 Greek New Testament manuscripts, some within a few generations of the originals, which lets scholars reconstruct the original text with remarkable confidence.',overview:['The "telephone game" picture is misleading. Copying did not happen in a single line, one person whispering to the next. It happened in many branches across many regions, so errors in one line can be caught by comparing others.','This field is called textual criticism. Scholars such as Wesley Huff, Daniel Wallace and Peter Gurry specialize in it, and it is one of the strongest areas of evidence for the Bible\'s reliability.'],points:[{heading:'An abundance of manuscripts',body:'Over 5,800 Greek manuscripts of the New Testament survive, alongside roughly 10,000 in Latin and thousands more in other ancient languages. By comparison, Homer\'s Iliad, the next best attested work, has around 1,900, and many classical works survive in a handful of copies.'},{heading:'Early copies',body:'Papyrus 52, a fragment of John 18, is commonly dated to the second century. Substantial papyri like P46 (Paul\'s letters) and P66 and P75 (John and Luke) are dated around AD 200 or earlier. For many classical authors the gap is closer to a thousand years.'},{heading:'Most variants are trivial',body:'The large number of variants is a result of having so many manuscripts. The vast majority are spelling differences, word order (which rarely matters in Greek) or obvious slips. Only a small fraction are both meaningful and viable, and no core Christian doctrine depends on a disputed reading.'},{heading:'The Old Testament too',body:'The Dead Sea Scrolls, discovered from 1947, include a nearly complete Isaiah scroll from roughly the second century BC, about a thousand years older than the previous oldest Hebrew copies. The text matches the later copies to a striking degree.'},{heading:'Early quotations',body:'The church fathers quoted the New Testament so extensively in sermons, letters and commentaries that much of it could be reconstructed from their quotations alone.'}],objections:[{objection:'There are more variants than words in the New Testament!',response:'That is true, and it reflects the wealth of manuscripts, not their weakness. If a book survived in one copy there would be zero variants, but also no way to check it. More copies mean more variants and more certainty about the original.'},{objection:'What about passages like the woman caught in adultery (John 7:53-8:11) or the long ending of Mark?',response:'Modern Bibles openly flag these in brackets or footnotes precisely because scholars can identify later additions. That transparency is a sign that the process works, and neither passage changes any Christian doctrine.'}],verses:[{ref:'Isaiah 40:8',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 24:35',quote:!0},{ref:'2 Timothy 3:16'}],searchTerms:'New Testament manuscripts reliability textual criticism',keyPassage:'2 Timothy 3',furtherReading:['Myths and Mistakes in New Testament Textual Criticism, ed. Elijah Hixson and Peter Gurry','Wesley Huff\'s teaching on manuscripts (wesleyhuff.com)','The Text of the New Testament by Bruce Metzger and Bart Ehrman']},{id:'canon',category:'bible',title:'Who Picked the Books?',question:'Didn\'t a church council decide which books got into the Bible centuries later?',shortAnswer:'The church did not create the canon; it recognized the books that were already functioning as Scripture. Most of the New Testament was accepted widely by the second century, long before any council.',overview:['A popular story says the Emperor Constantine or the Council of Nicaea (AD 325) chose the books of the Bible. That is a myth. Nicaea dealt with the deity of Christ, not the canon.','The process was organic. Churches read the writings that came from the apostles and their close associates, and those books gradually became universally recognized.'],points:[{heading:'Early recognition',body:'Peter already refers to Paul\'s letters as Scripture (2 Peter 3:15-16). By the late second century writers like Irenaeus were using the four Gospels as a settled set, and the Muratorian Fragment lists most of the New Testament books.'},{heading:'Clear criteria',body:'Books were recognized if they were apostolic (connected to an apostle or eyewitness), orthodox (consistent with the faith handed down), and catholic (used widely by churches everywhere, not just by one group).'},{heading:'Later lists confirmed, not invented',body:'In AD 367 Athanasius listed exactly our 27 New Testament books. Regional councils at Hippo (393) and Carthage (397) affirmed that list. They were recognizing a consensus, not imposing a new one.'},{heading:'The "lost gospels" are late',body:'Texts like the Gospel of Thomas or the Gospel of Judas are generally dated to the second century or later, well after the eyewitness generation, and reflect Gnostic ideas foreign to first-century Judaism.'}],objections:[{objection:'Some books, like 2 Peter or Revelation, were debated.',response:'Yes, and that shows the church took the question seriously rather than rubber-stamping everything. The core of the New Testament, the Gospels, Acts and Paul\'s letters, was never seriously in doubt.'},{objection:'What about the Old Testament canon?',response:'Jesus referred to the Hebrew Scriptures as "the law of Moses, the prophets, and the psalms" (Luke 24:44), the three-part division of the Jewish Bible, and treated them as God\'s word.'}],verses:[{ref:'2 Peter 3:15-16'},{ref:'Luke 24:44'},{ref:'2 Timothy 3:16',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'canon of scripture who decided books of the Bible',keyPassage:'2 Peter 3',furtherReading:['The Question of Canon by Michael Kruger','Canon Revisited by Michael Kruger']},{id:'archaeology',category:'bible',title:'Archaeology and the Bible',question:'Is there any archaeological evidence for the Bible?',shortAnswer:'Yes, a great deal. Archaeology has repeatedly confirmed people, places and details mentioned in Scripture, including some that critics once considered fictional.',overview:['Archaeology cannot prove that God spoke, but it can test whether the biblical writers were careful about history. On point after point, they have proven reliable.','Several discoveries have overturned earlier skeptical claims that particular people or places never existed.'],points:[{heading:'The House of David',body:'Some scholars once doubted King David was a historical person. The Tel Dan Stele, discovered in 1993, is a ninth-century BC inscription that refers to the "House of David."'},{heading:'Pontius Pilate',body:'The Pilate Stone, found at Caesarea in 1961, bears an inscription naming Pontius Pilate as prefect of Judea, confirming his title as well as his existence.'},{heading:'Caiaphas the high priest',body:'In 1990 an ornate ossuary (bone box) inscribed with the name "Joseph son of Caiaphas" was found in Jerusalem, widely identified with the high priest who presided over Jesus\' trial.'},{heading:'John\'s Jerusalem',body:'John describes the Pool of Bethesda with five porticoes (John 5:2) and the Pool of Siloam (John 9:7). Both have been excavated, the Siloam pool in 2004, confirming that the author knew pre-AD 70 Jerusalem well.'},{heading:'Luke the historian',body:'Luke uses the correct and sometimes unusual titles for local officials across the Roman world, such as "politarchs" in Thessalonica (Acts 17:6), a term later confirmed by inscriptions.'}],objections:[{objection:'Absence of evidence for events like the Exodus proves they didn\'t happen.',response:'Archaeology recovers only a tiny fraction of the past, and nomadic peoples leave little trace. Scholars debate the date and details of the Exodus, but repeated vindication of the Bible elsewhere should make us cautious about arguments from silence.'}],verses:[{ref:'John 5:2'},{ref:'Luke 3:1-2'},{ref:'Acts 17:6'}],searchTerms:'archaeology confirms the Bible',keyPassage:'Luke 3',furtherReading:['The Popular Handbook of Archaeology and the Bible, ed. Joseph Holden and Norman Geisler','On the Reliability of the Old Testament by K.A. Kitchen']},{id:'contradictions',category:'bible',title:'Alleged Contradictions',question:'Isn\'t the Bible full of contradictions?',shortAnswer:'Most alleged contradictions disappear when we read passages in context and understand how ancient authors wrote. Differences between accounts are often what we expect from independent eyewitnesses.',overview:['A contradiction means two statements cannot both be true at the same time and in the same sense. Many "contradictions" are really differences in emphasis, perspective or detail.','In fact, if four Gospel accounts agreed word for word, we would suspect collusion. Real witnesses tell the same story differently.'],points:[{heading:'Partial is not contradictory',body:'Matthew mentions one angel at the tomb, and Luke mentions two. If there were two, there was certainly one. Matthew is focusing on the one who spoke.'},{heading:'Ancient writing conventions',body:'Ancient historians often paraphrased speeches, arranged events topically rather than chronologically, and rounded numbers. Judging them by modern citation standards is anachronistic.'},{heading:'Different perspectives',body:'Matthew 27 says Judas hanged himself; Acts 1 says he fell and his body burst open. Both can be true if the body fell after the hanging. Likewise, Matthew says the priests bought the field; Acts says Judas "acquired" it because it was bought with his money.'},{heading:'Undesigned coincidences',body:'Sometimes one Gospel incidentally answers a question raised by another. For example, John 6 mentions Jesus asking Philip where to buy bread, and Luke 9 places the event near Bethsaida, which John 12:21 tells us was Philip\'s hometown. Such unplanned fits are marks of truthful testimony.'}],objections:[{objection:'Even one error means the Bible can\'t be God\'s word.',response:'Christians affirm the Bible is without error in what it teaches as originally written. When a genuine difficulty remains, the wise response is humility. Many "errors" once cited confidently have since been resolved by further study or discovery.'}],verses:[{ref:'Matthew 28:2-5'},{ref:'Luke 24:4'},{ref:'Proverbs 30:5',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'Bible contradictions answered',keyPassage:'Matthew 28',furtherReading:['Hidden in Plain View by Lydia McGrew (undesigned coincidences)','Big Book of Bible Difficulties by Norman Geisler and Thomas Howe']},{id:'prophecy',category:'bible',title:'Fulfilled Prophecy',question:'Did the Old Testament really predict Jesus?',shortAnswer:'Yes. Passages written centuries earlier describe the Messiah\'s birthplace, lineage, suffering, death and vindication. Some of those texts exist in copies that predate Jesus, so they could not have been written after the fact.',overview:['Jesus said the Scriptures testify about him (John 5:39), and after his resurrection he explained from Moses and all the prophets the things concerning himself (Luke 24:27).','The Dead Sea Scrolls show that key messianic prophecies, including Isaiah 53, existed in their current form before Jesus was born.'],points:[{heading:'The Suffering Servant (Isaiah 53)',body:'Written centuries before Christ, Isaiah 53 describes a righteous servant who is rejected, pierced for others\' transgressions, silent before his accusers, assigned a grave with the rich, and afterward sees life. The Great Isaiah Scroll from Qumran contains this chapter.'},{heading:'Psalm 22',body:'David describes a man who is mocked, whose hands and feet are pierced, whose garments are divided by lot, and who cries "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" Jesus quoted its first line on the cross.'},{heading:'Born in Bethlehem',body:'Micah 5:2 names Bethlehem as the birthplace of a ruler "whose goings out are from of old, from ancient times."'},{heading:'Timing and lineage',body:'The Messiah was to come from Abraham, Judah and David, and Daniel 9 connects his coming to the period before the destruction of the second temple, which fell in AD 70.'}],objections:[{objection:'Jesus or his followers could have deliberately fulfilled these prophecies.',response:'Some, perhaps. But no one can arrange their own birthplace, ancestry, the manner of their execution, or the actions of soldiers who gambled for their clothing.'},{objection:'Jewish interpreters say Isaiah 53 is about the nation of Israel.',response:'Israel is called God\'s servant elsewhere in Isaiah, but in Isaiah 53 the servant suffers innocently for the sins of "my people" (53:8), which distinguishes him from the nation. Several ancient Jewish sources also read the passage messianically.'}],verses:[{ref:'Isaiah 53:5',quote:!0},{ref:'Psalm 22:16-18'},{ref:'Micah 5:2'},{ref:'Luke 24:25-27'}],searchTerms:'messianic prophecies fulfilled by Jesus',keyPassage:'Isaiah 53',furtherReading:['Answering Jewish Objections to Jesus by Michael Brown','The Case for Christ by Lee Strobel']}]},856,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"buddhismTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const s=[{id:'buddhism-and-christianity',category:'buddhism',title:'Buddhism and Christianity',question:'Aren\'t Buddhism and Christianity basically teaching the same thing?',shortAnswer:'Both value compassion and self-control, but they give very different answers to life\'s biggest questions: whether there is a Creator, what is wrong with us, how we are rescued, and what our final hope is.',overview:['Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, lived in northern India around the fifth century BC. He taught the Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering (dukkha); suffering arises from craving; suffering can cease; and the Noble Eightfold Path leads to its cessation.','Buddhism takes many forms, from Theravada in Southeast Asia to Mahayana traditions like Zen and Pure Land. It is worth asking your friend what they personally believe rather than assuming.'],points:[{heading:'God',body:'The Buddha did not teach a personal Creator, and classical Buddhism treats the question as unhelpful to liberation. Christianity begins with a personal, loving God who made the world and wants to be known.'},{heading:'The problem',body:'Buddhism locates the root of suffering in craving and ignorance. Christianity agrees our desires are disordered, but says the deepest problem is sin: we have turned away from God, and we need forgiveness and reconciliation, not only insight.'},{heading:'The solution',body:'In Buddhism, liberation comes largely through one\'s own disciplined effort on the Eightfold Path. The Buddha is reported to have urged his followers to be lamps unto themselves. Jesus did not just point to a path; he said, "I am the way" (John 14:6), and offers rescue as a gift.'},{heading:'The goal',body:'Nirvana means the "blowing out" of craving and release from the cycle of rebirth. The Christian hope is not the extinguishing of the self but resurrection: a renewed life in a restored creation, in loving relationship with God forever.'},{heading:'The founder',body:'The Buddha taught a path and died. Christianity rests on the claim that Jesus rose from the dead, a historical claim that can be examined. See "The Resurrection."'}],objections:[{objection:'Jesus traveled to India and learned from Buddhists during his "lost years."',response:'This idea was popularized by Nicolas Notovitch\'s The Unknown Life of Jesus Christ (1894), which was quickly exposed as a fabrication. Jesus\' teaching is thoroughly rooted in the Hebrew Scriptures and first-century Judaism.'},{objection:'Buddhism is a philosophy, not a religion, so it is more scientific.',response:'Karma, rebirth and nirvana are metaphysical claims about reality that cannot be tested in a lab, just as Christian claims about God cannot. The fair question for both is which account best fits the evidence and our experience.'}],verses:[{ref:'John 14:6',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 11:28',quote:!0},{ref:'1 Corinthians 15:17',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'Buddhism compared to Christianity',keyPassage:'Matthew 11',furtherReading:['Jesus Among Other Gods by Ravi Zacharias','Neighboring Faiths by Winfried Corduan','Buddhism resources at carm.org']},{id:'karma-and-grace',category:'buddhism',title:'Karma or Grace?',question:'Isn\'t karma a fairer system than grace?',shortAnswer:'Karma says every action carries its consequences and cannot simply be forgiven. The gospel agrees that sin is serious, but announces that its debt has been paid by Christ, so forgiveness is possible and free.',overview:['In Buddhist and Hindu thought, karma is the principle that intentional actions produce results in this life and future lives. Beings are bound in samsara, a cycle of birth, death and rebirth, until liberation.','Christians share the conviction that moral choices truly matter. The difference is what happens to the moral debt we have already accumulated.'],points:[{heading:'Karma has no room for forgiveness',body:'Under strict karma, a wrong must be worked off. No one can bear it for you. Grace announces something karma cannot: God himself bore the consequences of our sin in Christ (Isaiah 53:5).'},{heading:'Justice and mercy together',body:'Grace does not pretend wrong doesn\'t matter. At the cross the debt is paid, not ignored. That is how God can be both perfectly just and truly merciful.'},{heading:'One life, then resurrection',body:'Rather than an endless cycle of rebirth, Scripture teaches that we live once and then face judgment (Hebrews 9:27), and that those in Christ will be raised to new life (John 11:25).'},{heading:'A longing for grace',body:'Pure Land Buddhism, one of the largest schools in East Asia, teaches reliance on the compassion of Amida Buddha rather than one\'s own effort. That longing for grace is deeply human. The gospel answers it with a historical person, Jesus of Nazareth, who died and rose again.'}],objections:[{objection:'Grace is unfair. People should face the consequences of their actions.',response:'The consequences are faced, but by Christ on our behalf. Grace is not fair in the sense of being deserved; it is better than fair. And it doesn\'t leave people unchanged: those forgiven much learn to love much (Luke 7:47).'},{objection:'The Bible teaches karma too: "a man reaps what he sows."',response:'Galatians 6:7 teaches that actions have consequences, but it sits within a relationship with a personal God who forgives. Karma is an impersonal law; the God of the Bible is a Father who runs to welcome the returning son (Luke 15:20).'}],verses:[{ref:'Ephesians 2:8-9',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 6:23',quote:!0},{ref:'Hebrews 9:27',quote:!0},{ref:'Galatians 6:7'},{ref:'Luke 15:11-24'}],searchTerms:'karma reincarnation versus grace',keyPassage:'Luke 15',furtherReading:['The Prodigal God by Timothy Keller','Jesus Among Other Gods by Ravi Zacharias']},{id:'no-self',category:'buddhism',title:'Is the Self an Illusion?',question:'Buddhism teaches there is no permanent self. How should a Christian respond?',shortAnswer:'The doctrine of "no-self" (anatta) is hard to live consistently: we experience ourselves as persons who think, choose, love and are responsible. Christianity affirms that persons are real and precious because they are made in the image of a personal God.',overview:['Buddhism teaches three marks of existence: impermanence, suffering and non-self. What we call a "self" is said to be a changing bundle of five aggregates (form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness), with no enduring core.','Recognizing that everything changes is wise. But concluding that persons are ultimately unreal raises difficult questions.'],points:[{heading:'Who becomes enlightened?',body:'If there is no self, it is hard to say who is suffering, who is practicing the path, and who is liberated. Buddhist thinkers answer with images of a continuous stream, like a flame passed from candle to candle, but the questions of identity and responsibility remain.'},{heading:'Love requires persons',body:'Love is always from someone to someone. If persons are illusions, love is too. Christianity says love is ultimate because God is eternally personal: Father, Son and Holy Spirit.'},{heading:'Human dignity',body:'Every person is made in God\'s image (Genesis 1:27) and "fearfully and wonderfully made" (Psalm 139:14). That is the foundation for human rights and for caring for the weak, whose worth doesn\'t depend on what they can do.'},{heading:'Transformed, not erased',body:'Christianity also calls us to die to the selfish self, but the goal is a new self, not no self: "if anyone is in Christ, the new creation has come" (2 Corinthians 5:17).'}],objections:[{objection:'Attachment causes suffering, so detachment is the wiser path.',response:'Attachment to the wrong things, like money or status, does cause suffering. But love always involves attachment. Jesus wept at the tomb of his friend Lazarus (John 11:35) and then raised him. The answer to grief is not ceasing to love but hope in the resurrection.'},{objection:'Meditation has real benefits.',response:'Quiet reflection and attention can be very good. Christianity has a long tradition of meditation, but its focus is not emptiness; it is on God and his word (Psalm 1:2), who fills us rather than emptying us.'}],verses:[{ref:'Genesis 1:27',quote:!0},{ref:'2 Corinthians 5:17',quote:!0},{ref:'John 11:25-26',quote:!0},{ref:'John 11:35'},{ref:'Psalm 1:2'}],searchTerms:'Buddhism no self anatta Christianity',keyPassage:'John 11',furtherReading:['Neighboring Faiths by Winfried Corduan','The Universe Next Door by James Sire']}]},857,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"foundationsTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'what-is-apologetics',category:'foundations',title:'What Is Apologetics?',question:'Why should Christians defend their faith at all?',shortAnswer:'Apologetics comes from the Greek word apologia, "a reasoned defense." Scripture calls every believer to be ready to explain why they hope in Christ, and to do it with gentleness and respect.',overview:['The word "apologetics" has nothing to do with saying sorry. In the ancient world an apologia was the case a defendant made in court. Peter uses that word when he tells ordinary Christians to be ready to give a reason for their hope (1 Peter 3:15).','Apologetics serves three purposes: it removes intellectual obstacles for people who are seeking, it strengthens the confidence of believers, and it shows that following Jesus involves the whole person, heart and mind together.'],points:[{heading:'It is a biblical command',body:'Peter (1 Peter 3:15), Paul (Philippians 1:7, 16) and Jude (Jude 3) all call believers to defend and contend for the faith. Jesus himself appealed to evidence: his works, fulfilled Scripture and eyewitness testimony.'},{heading:'It follows the apostles\' example',body:'In Acts, Paul "reasoned" in synagogues and marketplaces, and in Athens he engaged philosophers on their own terms, quoting their poets (Acts 17). Early Christians like Justin Martyr and Athenagoras wrote formal defenses of the faith to Roman emperors.'},{heading:'It serves the gospel, not replaces it',body:'Arguments cannot make someone a Christian; only the Holy Spirit gives new life. But God often uses good reasons to clear away barriers so people can hear the message of Christ clearly.'},{heading:'Character matters as much as content',body:'Peter adds "with gentleness and respect." A winning argument delivered with contempt can lose a person. The goal is not to win a debate but to point someone to Jesus.'}],objections:[{objection:'Faith shouldn\'t need evidence. Isn\'t apologetics a lack of trust?',response:'Biblical faith is trust grounded in what God has shown to be true, not a leap in the dark. Luke wrote so that his reader could "know the certainty of the things you have been taught" (Luke 1:4), and John wrote so that readers "may believe" (John 20:31). Evidence and trust work together.'},{objection:'No one ever came to faith through an argument.',response:'Many have. C.S. Lewis, Lee Strobel, J. Warner Wallace and countless others describe how evidence played a central role. Even when arguments are not the final step, they often remove the objection that kept someone from considering Christ.'}],verses:[{ref:'1 Peter 3:15',quote:!0},{ref:'Colossians 4:5-6',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 17:2-3'},{ref:'Jude 3'},{ref:'2 Corinthians 10:5'}],searchTerms:'what is Christian apologetics',keyPassage:'Acts 17',furtherReading:['Tactics by Greg Koukl','Mere Christianity by C.S. Lewis','Reasonable Faith by William Lane Craig']},{id:'faith-and-evidence',category:'foundations',title:'Faith and Evidence',question:'Isn\'t faith just believing something without evidence?',shortAnswer:'Not in the Bible. The New Testament word for faith (pistis) means trust or confidence, and it is consistently tied to evidence: eyewitness testimony, fulfilled prophecy and the resurrection.',overview:['A popular definition treats faith as "believing what you know isn\'t true" or believing despite the evidence. That is not how Scripture uses the word. Biblical faith is closer to how you trust a friend who has proven reliable.','Christianity is unusual among religions in resting on public, historical claims. Paul says plainly that if Christ has not been raised, the Christian faith is worthless (1 Corinthians 15:14). That is a claim that invites investigation.'],points:[{heading:'Faith is trust in a trustworthy object',body:'The strength of faith depends on what it rests on. Strong faith in thin ice still breaks through; weak faith in thick ice still holds. Christian faith rests on the character and actions of God in history.'},{heading:'The Bible appeals to evidence',body:'Jesus offered "many proofs" of his resurrection over forty days (Acts 1:3). Peter insists the apostles did not follow "cleverly devised stories" but were eyewitnesses (2 Peter 1:16). John records signs "that you may believe" (John 20:31).'},{heading:'Everyone lives by faith',body:'We trust historians about the past, doctors about medicine and scientists about the universe. The question is never whether to trust, but whether our trust is well placed.'}],objections:[{objection:'Hebrews 11:1 says faith is about things not seen.',response:'Unseen does not mean unevidenced. We have not seen gravity or electrons directly, yet we have good reasons to believe in them. Hebrews 11 goes on to list people whose trust rested on God\'s past faithfulness to them.'},{objection:'Jesus said "blessed are those who have not seen and yet believed."',response:'In John 20:29 Jesus is contrasting Thomas, who demanded to see personally, with later believers who would trust the reliable testimony of eyewitnesses. The very next verses say John wrote down those signs so readers could believe. Testimony is a form of evidence.'}],verses:[{ref:'John 20:30-31',quote:!0},{ref:'2 Peter 1:16',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 1:3'},{ref:'Hebrews 11:1'}],searchTerms:'faith and evidence blind faith',keyPassage:'John 20',furtherReading:['Cold-Case Christianity by J. Warner Wallace','Love Your God with All Your Mind by J.P. Moreland']},{id:'sharing-with-grace',category:'foundations',title:'Conversations That Persuade',question:'How do I talk about my faith without being pushy or getting stuck?',shortAnswer:'Ask good questions, listen carefully, and keep the focus on Jesus. You do not need to have every answer; you need to be honest, humble and curious.',overview:['Many Christians avoid spiritual conversations because they fear being asked a question they cannot answer. But good conversations are driven more by questions than by speeches.','Questions show respect, help you understand what the other person actually believes, and gently invite them to examine their own assumptions.'],points:[{heading:'"What do you mean by that?"',body:'Clarify before you respond. Words like "God," "faith," "Christian" or "truth" can mean very different things to different people. Understanding saves you from answering the wrong question.'},{heading:'"How did you come to that conclusion?"',body:'Every claim carries a burden of proof, including skeptical ones. Politely asking for reasons keeps the conversation fair and often reveals that a strong-sounding claim rests on little evidence.'},{heading:'It is fine to say "I don\'t know"',body:'Offer to look into it and follow up. That honesty builds trust, and a second conversation is often better than the first. The resources in this app are a great place to start.'},{heading:'Pray and point to Christ',body:'Arguments can clear the ground, but the heart of the message is a person: Jesus, crucified and risen. Keep bringing the conversation back to who he is and what he has done.'}],objections:[{objection:'Isn\'t it arrogant to try to convince someone they\'re wrong?',response:'If you believed a friend was about to cross a road into traffic, warning them would be love, not arrogance. Christians share because they believe the gospel is true and good. The manner matters: humility, patience and genuine care.'}],verses:[{ref:'Colossians 4:6',quote:!0},{ref:'2 Timothy 2:24-25'},{ref:'James 1:19'}],searchTerms:'how to share your faith evangelism',keyPassage:'2 Timothy 2',furtherReading:['Tactics by Greg Koukl','Questioning Evangelism by Randy Newman']}]},858,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"godTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return n}});const n=[{id:'kalam',category:'god',title:'The Universe Had a Beginning',question:'Why is there something rather than nothing?',shortAnswer:'Whatever begins to exist has a cause. The evidence strongly indicates the universe began to exist. So the universe has a cause beyond space, time and matter, which sounds a great deal like God.',overview:['This line of reasoning, known as the kalam cosmological argument, has roots in medieval Islamic and Christian philosophy and has been popularized today by William Lane Craig.','Its strength is that each premise is supported by ordinary intuition, philosophy and modern cosmology, and the conclusion follows logically.'],points:[{heading:'Premise 1: Whatever begins to exist has a cause',body:'Things do not pop into being uncaused out of nothing. If they could, it would be inexplicable why it doesn\'t happen all the time, with anything and everything. This premise is a first principle that even science assumes.'},{heading:'Premise 2: The universe began to exist',body:'Big Bang cosmology points to an absolute beginning of space and time. The Borde-Guth-Vilenkin theorem (2003) indicates that any universe which has on average been expanding cannot be past-eternal. The second law of thermodynamics adds that if the universe were infinitely old, it would already have run down.'},{heading:'Conclusion: The universe has a cause',body:'That cause must itself be beyond space and time (since it created them), immaterial, enormously powerful, and personal, because only a choice can explain how a timeless cause produces an effect with a beginning.'},{heading:'It fits Scripture',body:'Genesis 1:1 opens with an absolute beginning. Hebrews 11:3 says the visible universe was not made from visible things. Ancient Christians held this long before science confirmed a beginning.'}],objections:[{objection:'Then who made God?',response:'The premise is that whatever begins to exist has a cause. God, by definition, is eternal and never began to exist, so he does not need a cause. See the topic "Who Made God?" for more.'},{objection:'Quantum physics shows things can come from nothing.',response:'The quantum vacuum is not "nothing." It is a sea of energy governed by physical laws. Particles arising from it still arise from something. True nothing has no properties, no laws and no potential at all.'},{objection:'Maybe our universe came from a multiverse.',response:'That only pushes the question back. Most multiverse models are themselves subject to the Borde-Guth-Vilenkin result, so the larger system would still need a beginning.'}],verses:[{ref:'Genesis 1:1',quote:!0},{ref:'Hebrews 11:3',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 1:20',quote:!0},{ref:'Psalm 90:2'}],searchTerms:'kalam cosmological argument universe beginning',keyPassage:'Genesis 1',furtherReading:['Reasonable Faith by William Lane Craig','On Guard by William Lane Craig']},{id:'fine-tuning',category:'god',title:'A Finely Tuned Universe',question:'Isn\'t life in the universe just a lucky accident?',shortAnswer:'The laws and constants of physics fall within extraordinarily narrow ranges that permit life. The best explanation for such precise, life-permitting settings is a mind that intended them.',overview:['Physicists across worldviews acknowledge that if certain constants, such as the strength of gravity, the strong nuclear force or the cosmological constant, were even slightly different, no stable stars, chemistry or life could exist.','The fine-tuning argument asks what best explains this. There are three options: physical necessity, chance, or design.'],points:[{heading:'The precision is staggering',body:'By some estimates the cosmological constant is tuned to roughly one part in 10^120. The ratio of the strong nuclear force to the electromagnetic force, and the initial entropy of the universe, are also finely balanced.'},{heading:'Not physical necessity',body:'Nothing we know of in physics requires the constants to have the values they do. They appear to be independent of the laws themselves, like dials that could have been set differently.'},{heading:'Not plausibly chance',body:'The odds are so small that appealing to chance becomes unreasonable. Imagine a card player dealt a royal flush fifty hands in a row; you would rightly conclude the deck was arranged.'},{heading:'Design is the best explanation',body:'We already know that minds can set conditions to achieve purposes. A transcendent, intelligent Creator explains why the universe is fit for life. As Psalm 19:1 says, "The heavens declare the glory of God."'}],objections:[{objection:'If it weren\'t fine-tuned we wouldn\'t be here to notice.',response:'True, but that does not remove the need for an explanation. If you survived a firing squad of fifty marksmen who all missed, you should not shrug and say "if they had hit me I wouldn\'t be here." You should ask why they all missed.'},{objection:'A multiverse would make our universe inevitable somewhere.',response:'There is no direct evidence for a multiverse, and any mechanism that generates universes would itself need fine-tuning. It also risks explaining too much: it could "explain" any improbable event, which undermines ordinary science.'}],verses:[{ref:'Psalm 19:1',quote:!0},{ref:'Isaiah 45:18'},{ref:'Jeremiah 33:25'},{ref:'Colossians 1:17',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'fine tuning universe design argument',keyPassage:'Psalm 19',furtherReading:['A Fortunate Universe by Geraint Lewis and Luke Barnes','The Privileged Planet by Gonzalez and Richards']},{id:'moral-argument',category:'god',title:'The Moral Law',question:'Can we be good without God?',shortAnswer:'Atheists can certainly live moral lives. The deeper question is whether, without God, anything is truly right or wrong. Objective moral values point to a moral Lawgiver.',overview:['Nearly everyone recognizes that some things, such as torturing a child for fun, are really wrong, not just unfashionable or disadvantageous. That recognition is a clue to the nature of reality.','The moral argument reasons: If God does not exist, objective moral values do not exist. Objective moral values do exist. Therefore God exists.'],points:[{heading:'The question is grounding, not behavior',body:'The argument does not claim that unbelievers cannot know or do right. Paul says the law is written on every human heart (Romans 2:15). The claim is that only God provides a foundation for moral facts that are binding on everyone.'},{heading:'Without God, morality becomes opinion',body:'If humans are merely the products of blind evolution, our moral feelings are just survival instincts. They might be useful, but they would not be true. Many atheist philosophers have acknowledged this conclusion.'},{heading:'We experience morality as real',body:'When we say the Holocaust was evil, we don\'t mean "I dislike it." We mean it was wrong even though its perpetrators approved of it. Moral reformers like William Wilberforce and Martin Luther King Jr. appealed to a standard above their cultures.'},{heading:'God\'s nature is the standard',body:'In Christianity, goodness is not an arbitrary rule God invents; it flows from his unchanging, perfectly good character. That provides a stable, personal foundation for morality.'}],objections:[{objection:'Is something good because God commands it, or does God command it because it is good? (Euthyphro)',response:'This is a false dilemma. The good is grounded in God\'s own nature. His commands flow from who he is, so they are neither arbitrary nor dependent on some standard outside him.'},{objection:'Evolution explains why we have moral feelings.',response:'At most, evolution could explain why we have certain feelings, not whether those feelings track real moral truth. In fact, if our morality is only a survival mechanism, that gives us reason to doubt it is objectively true.'}],verses:[{ref:'Romans 2:14-15',quote:!0},{ref:'Micah 6:8'},{ref:'Psalm 145:17'}],searchTerms:'moral argument objective morality God',keyPassage:'Romans 2',furtherReading:['Mere Christianity by C.S. Lewis (Book 1)','Good God by David Baggett and Jerry Walls']},{id:'who-made-god',category:'god',title:'Who Made God?',question:'If everything needs a cause, what caused God?',shortAnswer:'Not everything needs a cause, only things that begin to exist. God is eternal and never began, so asking "who made God?" is like asking "who is the bachelor\'s wife?"',overview:['This is one of the most common questions children and skeptics ask, and it is a good one. It rests, however, on a misunderstanding of the arguments for God.','No serious argument claims that "everything has a cause." The claim is that everything that comes into being has a cause.'],points:[{heading:'An infinite chain explains nothing',body:'If every cause needed a prior cause forever, nothing would ever get started. Reason points to a first, uncaused cause that exists by its own nature.'},{heading:'God is necessary, not contingent',body:'Things like trees, planets and people exist but might not have. God, as the greatest possible being, exists necessarily. He depends on nothing outside himself.'},{heading:'Everyone believes in something eternal',body:'Either something has always existed, or everything came from absolutely nothing. The atheist has historically said the universe is eternal; the Christian says God is. Science now indicates the universe had a beginning.'}],objections:[{objection:'Richard Dawkins says God would have to be more complex than the universe, so even more improbable.',response:'God is not a physical object made of parts, so he is not complex in that sense. Also, in science we do not need an explanation of the explanation before accepting it. Archaeologists can recognize arrowheads as designed without knowing who designed the designer.'}],verses:[{ref:'Psalm 90:2',quote:!0},{ref:'Exodus 3:14'},{ref:'Revelation 1:8'}],searchTerms:'who made God who created God',keyPassage:'Psalm 90',furtherReading:['Who Made God? edited by Ravi Zacharias and Norman Geisler']}]},859,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"islamTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const s=[{id:'islam-and-jesus',category:'islam',title:'Islam and the Crucifixion',question:'Muslims honor Jesus as a prophet but say he wasn\'t crucified. Who is right?',shortAnswer:'The crucifixion of Jesus is one of the most firmly established facts of ancient history, attested by Christian, Roman and Jewish sources. The Qur\'an\'s denial comes about six hundred years later.',overview:['Islam holds Jesus (Isa) in high regard as a prophet born of a virgin who performed miracles. But Surah 4:157 states that he was not killed or crucified; it only appeared so.','Conversations with Muslim friends go best when we show respect for their devotion and focus on the historical evidence and on who Jesus claimed to be.'],points:[{heading:'Historians agree on the crucifixion',body:'It is reported in all four Gospels, in Paul\'s letters, by Tacitus and by Josephus. Scholars across the spectrum, including skeptics like Bart Ehrman and John Dominic Crossan, consider it certain.'},{heading:'Earliest sources carry the most weight',body:'Historians favor sources closest to events. The New Testament documents were written within decades, many by eyewitnesses or their associates. The Qur\'an was recited around AD 610-632, far removed from first-century Judea.'},{heading:'The Qur\'an affirms earlier Scripture',body:'The Qur\'an says the Torah and Gospel were given by God (for example Surah 5:46-47) and tells Christians to judge by the Gospel. Manuscripts from before Muhammad\'s time show that the Gospel then was the same one we have now, including the crucifixion and resurrection.'},{heading:'The cross is the heart of the message',body:'Jesus repeatedly predicted his death and resurrection (Mark 8:31). Without the cross there is no atonement and no resurrection, which is why the question matters so much.'}],objections:[{objection:'The Bible has been corrupted.',response:'This claim is hard to reconcile with the manuscript evidence, which shows no wholesale change to the text before or after Muhammad. It also raises a theological problem: if God could preserve the Qur\'an, why could he not preserve the Torah and Gospel he gave earlier?'},{objection:'God would not allow a great prophet to be humiliated on a cross.',response:'Scripture foresaw a suffering Messiah (Isaiah 53). The cross is not God\'s defeat but his victory: he vindicated Jesus by raising him from the dead.'}],verses:[{ref:'Mark 8:31'},{ref:'Isaiah 53:5',quote:!0},{ref:'1 Corinthians 15:3-4'},{ref:'Galatians 1:8'}],searchTerms:'Islam Jesus crucifixion Quran 4:157',keyPassage:'Mark 15',furtherReading:['Seeking Allah, Finding Jesus by Nabeel Qureshi','No God but One by Nabeel Qureshi','Islam resources at carm.org']},{id:'islam-bible-corrupted',category:'islam',title:'Was the Bible Corrupted?',question:'Muslims often say the Torah and Gospel were changed. Is that true?',shortAnswer:'The Qur\'an itself affirms the Torah and Gospel, tells Christians to judge by them, and says no one can change God\'s words. And manuscripts from centuries before Muhammad show the Bible we read today.',overview:['Many Muslims believe the Bible has undergone tahrif (corruption), which is why it disagrees with the Qur\'an about the crucifixion and the deity of Christ. This belief is sincere and important to understand.','A respectful way to engage is to look at what the Qur\'an itself says about earlier Scripture, and then at the historical evidence.'],points:[{heading:'The Qur\'an affirms the Gospel',body:'The Qur\'an says God gave Jesus the Gospel (Injil) as guidance and light, and tells "the People of the Gospel" to judge by what God revealed in it (Surah 5:46-47). It tells Muhammad that if he is in doubt he should ask those who were reading the Scripture before him (10:94). It even says Jews and Christians have nothing to stand on until they uphold the Torah and the Gospel (5:68).'},{heading:'God\'s words cannot be changed',body:'The Qur\'an states repeatedly that none can alter the words of God (6:34, 6:115, 18:27). If God is able to preserve the Qur\'an, it would be strange to say he could not, or would not, preserve the Torah and Gospel he also sent.'},{heading:'The manuscripts predate Muhammad',body:'Muhammad lived from about AD 570 to 632. Complete Bibles such as Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus date to the fourth century, and papyri and the Dead Sea Scrolls are older still. They contain the same Gospel we have now, including Jesus\' crucifixion, resurrection and claims to be the Son of God.'},{heading:'A dilemma',body:'If the Bible was corrupted before Muhammad, why would the Qur\'an tell Christians of his day to judge by it? If it was corrupted after, the earlier manuscripts would show the change, and they don\'t. Either way, the corruption claim is hard to sustain.'}],objections:[{objection:'Paul corrupted the message of Jesus.',response:'Paul\'s letters are our earliest Christian documents, and he records that Peter, James and John, Jesus\' closest followers, recognized his gospel and gave him "the right hand of fellowship" (Galatians 2:9). The Gospels, written independently, contain the same core message: Jesus\' death for sins, his resurrection and his divine identity.'},{objection:'Scholars admit some verses were added later, like 1 John 5:7.',response:'Yes, and they know that because the thousands of manuscripts make later additions easy to spot. Modern translations footnote or omit them. Detectable, minor variations are very different from a wholesale rewriting of the message, which the evidence does not show.'}],verses:[{ref:'Isaiah 40:8',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 24:35',quote:!0},{ref:'Psalm 119:89',quote:!0},{ref:'Galatians 2:9'}],searchTerms:'was the Bible corrupted Islam tahrif',keyPassage:'Galatians 2',furtherReading:['Answering Islam by Norman Geisler and Abdul Saleeb','The Bible Among the Myths by John Oswalt','Islam resources at carm.org']},{id:'islam-son-of-god',category:'islam',title:'How Can God Have a Son?',question:'The Qur\'an says God "does not beget." Isn\'t calling Jesus the Son of God blasphemy?',shortAnswer:'Christians agree that God does not have a son through a wife or physical birth. "Son of God" describes an eternal relationship and a shared nature, not biology.',overview:['Surah 112 says God "neither begets nor is begotten," and Surah 6:101 asks how God could have a son when he has no companion. Many Muslims understand "Son of God" to mean that God had physical relations, which Christians also reject.','The Qur\'an also describes the Christian Trinity as God, Jesus and Mary (5:116). Clearing up these misunderstandings is often the most helpful first step.'],points:[{heading:'Not a biological term',body:'We use "son" figuratively all the time, as in "son of the soil." In Scripture, "Son of God" expresses that Jesus shares the Father\'s nature and has an eternal, unique relationship with him. It was never about a physical birth from God.'},{heading:'His hearers understood it as a claim to equality',body:'When Jesus called God his own Father, his opponents understood he was "making himself equal with God" (John 5:18). The title points to his divine identity, not to a created or procreated being.'},{heading:'The Trinity is not Father, Mother and Son',body:'Christians have never worshiped Mary as part of God. The Trinity is one God in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. Christians share the Muslim conviction that there is only one God.'},{heading:'A bridge in the Qur\'an',body:'The Qur\'an calls Jesus "a Word from God" and "a spirit from him" (4:171). The Gospel of John opens by saying the eternal Word, who was with God and was God, became flesh. This can be a fruitful starting point for conversation.'}],objections:[{objection:'It is beneath God\'s majesty to become a man.',response:'Muslims rightly affirm that God can do whatever he wills. The question is not whether God is able but whether he chose to. Christians see God\'s greatness shown precisely in his humility and love in coming to rescue us (Philippians 2:5-11).'},{objection:'Jesus never said, "I am God, worship me."',response:'He made the claim in ways his Jewish audience understood even more forcefully: forgiving sins, taking the divine name, claiming to share God\'s throne and accepting worship. See the topic "Is Jesus God?"'}],verses:[{ref:'Matthew 16:16',quote:!0},{ref:'Galatians 4:4',quote:!0},{ref:'John 1:1-3',quote:!0},{ref:'John 5:18'},{ref:'Psalm 2:7'}],searchTerms:'Son of God Islam Jesus begotten',keyPassage:'John 5',furtherReading:['No God but One by Nabeel Qureshi','Answering Islam by Norman Geisler and Abdul Saleeb']},{id:'islam-muhammad-in-bible',category:'islam',title:'Is Muhammad Foretold in the Bible?',question:'Don\'t Deuteronomy 18 and John 14 predict the coming of Muhammad?',shortAnswer:'Read in context, the "prophet like Moses" comes from among the Israelites and is identified with Jesus, and the Helper Jesus promised is the Holy Spirit, who would live inside his disciples.',overview:['The Qur\'an says Jesus foretold a messenger named Ahmad (Surah 61:6), and Muslim writers often point to Deuteronomy 18 and Jesus\' promise of the Paraclete (Helper) in John 14-16 as prophecies of Muhammad.','Taking these passages seriously means reading them in their own context.'],points:[{heading:'A prophet "from your fellow Israelites"',body:'Moses said God would raise up a prophet like him "from among you, from your fellow Israelites" (Deuteronomy 18:15). "Brothers" in this passage refers to the tribes of Israel (compare 17:15). Peter applies this prophecy to Jesus (Acts 3:22-26).'},{heading:'Like Moses, face to face',body:'Deuteronomy 34:10-12 says no prophet had arisen like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face and who performed great signs. Jesus, who knew the Father intimately and performed signs and wonders, fits this description.'},{heading:'The Helper is the Holy Spirit',body:'Jesus explicitly names the Helper as "the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in my name" (John 14:26). He says the world cannot see him, that he "lives with you and will be in you," and will be with the disciples forever (14:16-17). He came within weeks at Pentecost (Acts 1:5, 2:1-4).'},{heading:'No manuscript support',body:'Some suggest the original Greek word was periklytos ("praised one," similar to Ahmad) rather than parakletos ("helper"). None of the thousands of Greek manuscripts of John reads periklytos.'}],objections:[{objection:'Song of Songs 5:16 names Muhammad ("machmadim").',response:'The Hebrew word is a common adjective meaning "desirable" or "lovely," used elsewhere for precious things (for example 1 Kings 20:6). In context a bride is describing her beloved. Reading a personal name into it would change the meaning of the whole poem.'},{objection:'Muhammad brought the same basic message as the earlier prophets.',response:'The Bible gives a test for later revelation: it must agree with what God has already revealed (Deuteronomy 13:1-4, Galatians 1:8). The Qur\'an denies the crucifixion and deity of Christ, which are central to the Gospel the Qur\'an says came from God.'}],verses:[{ref:'Deuteronomy 18:15',quote:!0},{ref:'John 14:26',quote:!0},{ref:'Galatians 1:8',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 3:22-26'},{ref:'John 14:16-17'}],searchTerms:'Muhammad prophesied in the Bible Deuteronomy 18 Paraclete',keyPassage:'John 14',furtherReading:['Answering Islam by Norman Geisler and Abdul Saleeb','Seeking Allah, Finding Jesus by Nabeel Qureshi']},{id:'islam-assurance',category:'islam',title:'Can I Know I\'m Saved?',question:'In Islam, good and bad deeds are weighed. How is the Christian hope different?',shortAnswer:'The Qur\'an describes deeds being weighed on the Day of Judgment, so no one can be sure of the outcome. The gospel offers assurance, because salvation rests on what Christ has done, not on how our scales balance.',overview:['Many Muslims live with sincere devotion and a real fear of judgment. The Qur\'an speaks of scales: those whose good deeds are heavy will prosper and those whose deeds are light will lose (7:8-9, 23:102-103).','Even Muhammad is recorded saying, "I do not know what will be done with me or with you" (46:9). This is a place where the good news of grace speaks directly to a real longing.'],points:[{heading:'Good deeds cannot erase guilt',body:'A judge cannot acquit a guilty criminal because he also did good things. Justice requires the wrong to be dealt with. Scripture says all have sinned (Romans 3:23), so our record cannot save us however many good deeds we add.'},{heading:'God\'s justice and mercy meet at the cross',body:'God does not simply overlook sin; he paid its penalty himself in Christ. That is how he can be both just and the one who justifies (Romans 3:26).'},{heading:'Salvation is a gift',body:'Salvation is received by grace through faith, not earned by works (Ephesians 2:8-9). That is why John can write so that believers "may know" that they have eternal life (1 John 5:13).'},{heading:'A bridge: the great sacrifice',body:'The Qur\'an says God ransomed Abraham\'s son "with a great sacrifice" (37:107). That story points to the principle of substitution, which finds its fulfillment in Jesus, "the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world" (John 1:29).'}],objections:[{objection:'If salvation is free, people will just keep sinning.',response:'Paul anticipated this: "Shall we go on sinning so that grace may increase? By no means!" (Romans 6:1-2). Grace does not just forgive; it transforms. Good works flow from gratitude, not fear.'},{objection:'God can forgive whomever he wants without a sacrifice.',response:'Forgiveness always costs the one who forgives. A just God cannot pretend evil does not matter. At the cross God bears the cost himself, so that forgiveness is both free to us and just.'}],verses:[{ref:'Ephesians 2:8-9',quote:!0},{ref:'1 John 5:13',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 6:23',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 3:23-26'},{ref:'John 1:29'}],searchTerms:'assurance of salvation Islam grace works',keyPassage:'Ephesians 2',furtherReading:['Seeking Allah, Finding Jesus by Nabeel Qureshi','The Cross of Christ by John Stott']}]},860,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"jwTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const s=[{id:'jehovahs-witnesses',category:'jw',title:'Talking with Jehovah\'s Witnesses',question:'Jehovah\'s Witnesses say Jesus is "a god," Michael the archangel. How do I respond?',shortAnswer:'Be kind, and keep coming back to the text of Scripture, especially passages that show Jesus sharing the identity, works and worship of Jehovah.',overview:['Jehovah\'s Witnesses are often sincere and well prepared. They teach that Jesus is a created being, the first creation of Jehovah, and that the Holy Spirit is an impersonal force. They use their own New World Translation.','The most helpful conversations are patient, respectful and focused on a few key passages read carefully together.'],points:[{heading:'John 1:1 and "a god"',body:'The New World Translation renders John 1:1c as "the Word was a god." In Greek, a predicate noun placed before the verb without the article typically describes the nature or quality of the subject rather than making it indefinite. The sense is "what God was, the Word was." A "lesser god" would also conflict with strict monotheism (Isaiah 43:10).'},{heading:'Jesus is the Creator',body:'John 1:3 says "through him all things were made," and Colossians 1:16 says all things were created by him. Isaiah 44:24 says Jehovah made all things alone. Only the Creator can be the agent of all creation. The NWT inserts the word "other" into Colossians 1 ("all other things") to avoid this.'},{heading:'Jesus receives worship',body:'In Hebrews 1:6 the angels are commanded to worship the Son. Angels refuse worship (Revelation 22:8-9), so the Son cannot be an angel, and Hebrews 1 explicitly contrasts him with angels.'},{heading:'Jehovah texts applied to Jesus',body:'Isaiah 45:23 says every knee will bow to Jehovah; Philippians 2:10-11 applies this to Jesus. Joel 2:32 says whoever calls on the name of Jehovah will be saved; Romans 10:9-13 applies it to Jesus.'}],objections:[{objection:'Colossians 1:15 calls Jesus "the firstborn of all creation," so he was created.',response:'"Firstborn" can mean preeminent heir, not first created. God calls David his "firstborn" (Psalm 89:27), though David was the youngest son. The next verse says all things were created by him, which rules out his being one of them.'}],verses:[{ref:'John 1:1-3',quote:!0},{ref:'Colossians 1:15-17'},{ref:'Hebrews 1:6-8'},{ref:'Isaiah 44:24'},{ref:'Philippians 2:9-11'}],searchTerms:'Jehovah\'s Witnesses deity of Christ John 1:1',keyPassage:'Hebrews 1',furtherReading:['Reasoning from the Scriptures with the Jehovah\'s Witnesses by Ron Rhodes','Jehovah\'s Witnesses resources at carm.org']},{id:'jw-holy-spirit',category:'jw',title:'Is the Holy Spirit a Person?',question:'Jehovah\'s Witnesses say the Holy Spirit is God\'s "active force." What does the Bible say?',shortAnswer:'Scripture describes the Holy Spirit doing what only a person can do: speaking, teaching, deciding, being grieved and being lied to. A force cannot do any of these.',overview:['Jehovah\'s Witnesses teach that the Holy Spirit is not a person but an impersonal energy, God\'s "active force," like electricity.','The clearest way to respond is to read passages together that describe what the Spirit does.'],points:[{heading:'The Spirit speaks and decides',body:'The Spirit said, "Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them" (Acts 13:2). He distributes gifts "just as he determines" (1 Corinthians 12:11). Forces do not speak in the first person or make choices.'},{heading:'The Spirit can be grieved',body:'Paul writes, "do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God" (Ephesians 4:30). You cannot hurt the feelings of electricity.'},{heading:'Lying to the Spirit is lying to God',body:'Peter tells Ananias he has lied to the Holy Spirit and then says, "You have not lied just to human beings but to God" (Acts 5:3-4). The Spirit is both personal and divine.'},{heading:'"He," not "it"',body:'In John 14-16 Jesus calls the Spirit "another Helper" like himself, who teaches, reminds, testifies and guides. John uses the masculine pronoun "he" (ekeinos) even though the Greek word for spirit (pneuma) is neuter.'}],objections:[{objection:'The Spirit is "poured out" and people are "filled" with it, so it must be a force.',response:'These are metaphors, and Scripture uses similar language of persons: believers are told to "clothe yourselves with the Lord Jesus Christ" (Romans 13:14), and Christ "fills everything in every way" (Ephesians 1:23). Metaphors do not cancel the many passages that show the Spirit acting as a person.'},{objection:'The Holy Spirit has no personal name.',response:'Jesus commands baptism in "the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit" (Matthew 28:19), placing the Spirit alongside the Father and Son in a single name.'}],verses:[{ref:'Ephesians 4:30',quote:!0},{ref:'John 16:13',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 5:3-4'},{ref:'Acts 13:2'},{ref:'1 Corinthians 12:11'}],searchTerms:'Holy Spirit person Jehovah\'s Witnesses active force',keyPassage:'John 16',furtherReading:['Reasoning from the Scriptures with the Jehovah\'s Witnesses by Ron Rhodes','The Forgotten Trinity by James White']},{id:'jw-predictions',category:'jw',title:'The Watchtower\'s Predictions',question:'How should I think about the Watchtower organization\'s claims to speak for God?',shortAnswer:'The organization has repeatedly announced dates for the end of the world, including 1914, 1925 and 1975, that did not come true. Scripture gives a clear test for anyone who claims to speak for God, and urges every believer to check teaching against the Bible itself.',overview:['Jehovah\'s Witnesses are led by a Governing Body in the Watch Tower Bible and Tract Society, which since 2013 has identified itself as the "faithful and discreet slave" of Matthew 24:45, the channel through which God gives spiritual food to his people.','Raise this topic gently. Many Witnesses have never been shown the history, and it can be painful to consider.'],points:[{heading:'A history of failed dates',body:'Founder Charles Taze Russell taught that 1914 would see the end of the present order. Under Joseph Rutherford, the campaign "Millions Now Living Will Never Die" pointed to 1925, including the return of the Old Testament patriarchs. In the late 1960s and early 1970s, publications pointed to 1975 as the end of 6,000 years of human history.'},{heading:'The biblical test',body:'Deuteronomy 18:22 says that if what someone proclaims in the Lord\'s name does not come true, the Lord has not spoken it. Jesus himself said no one knows the day or hour (Matthew 24:36).'},{heading:'"New light" or changing teaching?',body:'The organization explains changes as "new light," citing Proverbs 4:18. But truth that God reveals does not reverse itself, and the organization has at times required members to accept teachings that it later abandoned.'},{heading:'Be like the Bereans',body:'The Bereans were commended for examining the Scriptures to see if what even the apostle Paul said was true (Acts 17:11). Encourage your friend to read the Bible for themselves and to ask what it says about Jesus.'}],objections:[{objection:'The organization never claimed to be an inspired prophet.',response:'It claims to be God\'s sole channel of truth on earth, and a 1972 issue of The Watchtower described the anointed class as a "prophet" among the nations. If an organization claims to speak for God, it is fair to apply the Bible\'s test.'},{objection:'Christians have made failed predictions too.',response:'That is true, and they were wrong as well. The difference is that mainstream Christianity has never made obedience to a particular organization\'s date-setting a condition of being right with God.'}],verses:[{ref:'Deuteronomy 18:22',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 24:36',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 17:11',quote:!0},{ref:'Proverbs 4:18'}],searchTerms:'Jehovah\'s Witnesses false prophecies 1914 1975',keyPassage:'Deuteronomy 18',furtherReading:['Crisis of Conscience by Raymond Franz (former Governing Body member)','Jehovah\'s Witnesses resources at carm.org']},{id:'jw-bodily-resurrection',category:'jw',title:'Did Jesus Rise in His Body?',question:'Jehovah\'s Witnesses say Jesus was raised as a spirit creature. Does the Bible agree?',shortAnswer:'No. Jesus predicted he would raise his own body, and after the resurrection he showed his wounds, ate with his disciples and said, "a ghost does not have flesh and bones, as you see I have."',overview:['Watchtower teaching holds that Jesus was raised as an invisible spirit, and that God disposed of his physical body. His appearances are explained as materialized bodies he took on temporarily.','The Gospels go out of their way to show that the risen Jesus was physical and was the same body that had been crucified.'],points:[{heading:'Jesus predicted it',body:'Jesus said, "Destroy this temple, and I will raise it again in three days." John explains that "the temple he had spoken of was his body" (John 2:19-21). The same body that was destroyed would be raised.'},{heading:'Flesh and bones',body:'When the disciples thought they were seeing a spirit, Jesus corrected them: "Touch me and see; a ghost does not have flesh and bones, as you see I have" (Luke 24:39). He then ate fish in front of them.'},{heading:'The same wounds',body:'Jesus invited Thomas to put his finger in the nail marks and his hand in his side (John 20:27). A temporarily materialized body would not have the wounds of the crucified one.'},{heading:'The tomb was empty',body:'If Jesus had been raised only as a spirit, there would be no need for an empty tomb. All four Gospels emphasize that the body was gone and the grave clothes left behind.'}],objections:[{objection:'1 Corinthians 15:44 says the body is raised a "spiritual body."',response:'Paul uses "spiritual" to mean empowered and directed by God\'s Spirit, not immaterial. In 1 Corinthians 2:15 the "spiritual" person is still a flesh-and-blood human. The contrast is between a body subject to weakness and death, and a body transformed by the Spirit.'},{objection:'"Flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God" (1 Corinthians 15:50).',response:'"Flesh and blood" is a Jewish expression for mortal human nature. The very next verses explain that the perishable must be clothed with the imperishable: the body is transformed, not discarded.'}],verses:[{ref:'Luke 24:39',quote:!0},{ref:'John 2:19',quote:!0},{ref:'John 20:27'},{ref:'1 Corinthians 15:42-44'}],searchTerms:'bodily resurrection of Jesus Jehovah\'s Witnesses spirit creature',keyPassage:'Luke 24',furtherReading:['Reasoning from the Scriptures with the Jehovah\'s Witnesses by Ron Rhodes','The Resurrection of the Son of God by N.T. Wright']},{id:'jw-144000',category:'jw',title:'The 144,000 and the Hope of Heaven',question:'Is heaven only for 144,000 people, with everyone else living on earth?',shortAnswer:'The Bible promises every believer the same standing as God\'s child and the same hope. Revelation itself shows a "great multitude that no one could count" standing before God\'s throne.',overview:['Jehovah\'s Witnesses teach that only 144,000 "anointed" Christians go to heaven to rule with Christ. The "great crowd" of other faithful Witnesses hope to live forever on a paradise earth, and only the anointed partake of the bread and wine at the annual Memorial.','Christians also believe in a renewed earth (Revelation 21). The disagreement is about dividing believers into two classes with different relationships to God.'],points:[{heading:'Read Revelation 7 carefully',body:'The 144,000 are described as 12,000 from each of the twelve tribes of Israel (7:4-8). Immediately after, John sees a great multitude from every nation standing "before the throne and before the Lamb" (7:9) and serving God "in his temple" (7:15).'},{heading:'Consistent interpretation',body:'If the number 144,000 is literal, it is fair to ask whether the other details are too: that they are all Israelites from specific tribes and, according to Revelation 14:4, all people who "did not defile themselves with women."'},{heading:'All believers are God\'s children',body:'John 1:12 says all who receive Christ are given the right to become children of God. Paul says "our citizenship is in heaven" (Philippians 3:20), and Jesus commanded all his disciples to remember him with the bread and cup (Luke 22:19).'},{heading:'One hope',body:'Paul writes that there is "one hope" for the whole church (Ephesians 4:4). Every believer is born again and united with Christ, with no second class of Christians.'}],objections:[{objection:'Jesus spoke of a "little flock" (Luke 12:32), which means a small group goes to heaven.',response:'In context Jesus is comforting his disciples, who were few at that time, not setting a limit on the number of those who would be saved. In John 10:16 the "other sheep" are brought into "one flock" with one shepherd, most naturally Gentiles joining Jewish believers, not a separate earthly class.'}],verses:[{ref:'Revelation 7:9',quote:!0},{ref:'John 1:12',quote:!0},{ref:'Philippians 3:20',quote:!0},{ref:'Revelation 7:4-8'},{ref:'Ephesians 4:4-6'}],searchTerms:'144000 Jehovah\'s Witnesses great crowd heaven',keyPassage:'Revelation 7',furtherReading:['Reasoning from the Scriptures with the Jehovah\'s Witnesses by Ron Rhodes','Jehovah\'s Witnesses resources at carm.org']}]},861,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"ldsTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'lds-talking-with',category:'lds',title:'Talking with Latter-day Saints',question:'Mormons talk about Jesus, grace and the gospel. Don\'t we believe the same things?',shortAnswer:'Latter-day Saints use many of the same words as Christians, but often with very different meanings. Kind, patient conversations that define terms and return to the Bible are the most fruitful.',overview:['The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints was founded by Joseph Smith in 1830. Its members are often devout, family-centered and generous, and many serve as missionaries. They sincerely see themselves as Christians.','Alongside the Bible, they accept the Book of Mormon, the Doctrine and Covenants and the Pearl of Great Price as Scripture, and believe in continuing revelation through a living prophet.'],points:[{heading:'Same words, different dictionary',body:'Ask what your friend means by "God," "Jesus," "grace," "salvation" and "heaven." In Latter-day Saint teaching, Jesus is the spirit brother of all people, God the Father has a physical body, and exaltation involves becoming like God. Clarifying meanings avoids talking past each other.'},{heading:'Start with the Bible',body:'Latter-day Saints accept the Bible "as far as it is translated correctly" (Articles of Faith 8). It is common ground. Read key passages together and ask what they plainly say.'},{heading:'Share your assurance',body:'Many Latter-day Saints feel the weight of needing to be worthy. Simply sharing how you know your sins are forgiven because of what Christ has done, not what you have done, can be powerful (1 John 5:13).'},{heading:'Be patient and kind',body:'Leaving the church can cost a Latter-day Saint family relationships and community. Be a friend for the long haul, not just for one conversation.'}],objections:[{objection:'You can\'t judge my church; I have a testimony that it\'s true.',response:'Your sincerity is real, and I respect it. Feelings are important but can mislead all of us (Jeremiah 17:9). Scripture tells us to test what we hear against what God has already revealed (1 John 4:1, Acts 17:11), and that is all I\'m asking us to do together.'}],verses:[{ref:'1 John 4:1',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 17:11',quote:!0},{ref:'1 John 5:13',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'witnessing to Mormons Latter-day Saints',keyPassage:'1 John 4',furtherReading:['Mormonism 101 by Bill McKeever and Eric Johnson','The Mormon Mirage by Latayne Scott','Mormonism resources at carm.org']},{id:'lds-nature-of-god',category:'lds',title:'Was God Once a Man?',question:'Latter-day Saints teach that God the Father has a body and was once a man. What does the Bible say?',shortAnswer:'The Bible teaches that there is only one God, who has always been God, is spirit, does not change, and is not a man. This is the deepest difference between historic Christianity and Latter-day Saint teaching.',overview:['In the 1844 King Follett discourse, Joseph Smith taught that "God himself was once as we are now, and is an exalted man." Doctrine and Covenants 130:22 says the Father "has a body of flesh and bones as tangible as man\'s." Lorenzo Snow summarized: "As man now is, God once was; as God now is, man may be."','Some Latter-day Saints today are less certain about the details of God\'s past, but the teaching that God is an exalted, embodied being remains central.'],points:[{heading:'Only one God, ever',body:'Through Isaiah, God says: "Before me no god was formed, nor will there be one after me" (Isaiah 43:10). He knows of no other god (Isaiah 44:8). There is no room for gods before him or for people later becoming gods.'},{heading:'God is eternally God',body:'"From everlasting to everlasting you are God" (Psalm 90:2). God did not progress into deity; he has always been God and does not change (Malachi 3:6).'},{heading:'God is not a man',body:'"God is not human, that he should lie" (Numbers 23:19), and "God is spirit" (John 4:24). The Son became human in the incarnation, but the Father did not.'},{heading:'Why it matters',body:'If God was once a man who became God, he is not the uncaused Creator of all things but part of a larger universe. The Bible\'s God is the source of everything that exists.'}],objections:[{objection:'Genesis says we are made in God\'s image, so God must have a body.',response:'The image of God refers to qualities like reason, morality, relationship and dominion over creation, not a physical shape. Animals have bodies too, but they are not described as made in God\'s image.'},{objection:'Jesus quoted Psalm 82: "You are gods."',response:'Psalm 82 rebukes unjust human judges, called "gods" because they acted with God\'s authority, and warns that they "will die like mere mortals" (82:7). Jesus used it to rebuke his accusers, not to teach that people become gods (John 10:34-36).'}],verses:[{ref:'Isaiah 43:10',quote:!0},{ref:'Numbers 23:19',quote:!0},{ref:'John 4:24',quote:!0},{ref:'Psalm 90:2',quote:!0},{ref:'Malachi 3:6',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'Mormon God was once a man exalted',keyPassage:'Isaiah 43',furtherReading:['Mormonism 101 by Bill McKeever and Eric Johnson','The Forgotten Trinity by James White']},{id:'lds-book-of-mormon',category:'lds',title:'Is the Book of Mormon Historical?',question:'Is there evidence that the Book of Mormon describes real people and events?',shortAnswer:'The Book of Mormon describes large civilizations in the ancient Americas, but archaeology, genetics and history have not confirmed them, and it contains details that don\'t fit the period. This contrasts sharply with the Bible.',overview:['The Book of Mormon says it records Israelites who sailed to the Americas around 600 BC, built great cities and fought wars involving hundreds of thousands of people, before ending around AD 400.','Joseph Smith said he translated it from golden plates, which were returned to an angel and are not available to examine.'],points:[{heading:'No confirmed archaeology',body:'No Book of Mormon city, person or place name in the Americas has been confirmed by archaeology. Compare the Bible, where archaeology has confirmed many people and places (see "Archaeology and the Bible").'},{heading:'Anachronisms',body:'The text mentions horses, chariots, steel, silk, wheat and elephants in the ancient Americas. Horses, for example, had been extinct there for thousands of years before the Spanish brought them back.'},{heading:'DNA evidence',body:'Genetic studies show that Native American ancestry traces mainly to Asia. The church\'s own Gospel Topics essay "Book of Mormon and DNA Studies" acknowledges this, and in 2006 the book\'s introduction changed from calling the Lamanites the "principal ancestors" of Native Americans to "among the ancestors."'},{heading:'Changes to the text',body:'The book is called "the most correct of any book on earth," yet thousands of changes have been made since 1830. Some are doctrinal: 1 Nephi 11:18 originally called Mary "the mother of God," later changed to "the mother of the Son of God."'}],objections:[{objection:'Absence of evidence isn\'t evidence of absence.',response:'Over time, though, the absence becomes telling. The Book of Mormon describes millions of people, large cities and huge battles, which should leave traces. For the Bible, the evidence continues to accumulate.'},{objection:'Moroni 10:4 says to pray, and God will confirm it\'s true by the Holy Ghost.',response:'God never contradicts what he has already revealed. Scripture asks us to test new claims by God\'s existing word (Galatians 1:8, Acts 17:11), not to rely on feelings alone. A sincere feeling cannot make a historical claim true.'}],verses:[{ref:'Galatians 1:8',quote:!0},{ref:'Proverbs 30:6',quote:!0},{ref:'2 Peter 1:16',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'Book of Mormon archaeology evidence',keyPassage:'Galatians 1',furtherReading:['The Mormon Mirage by Latayne Scott','Losing a Lost Tribe by Thomas Murphy and Simon Southerton','The church\'s own Gospel Topics essays at churchofjesuschrist.org']},{id:'lds-great-apostasy',category:'lds',title:'Was the Church Lost?',question:'Latter-day Saints teach that the true church disappeared after the apostles. Did it?',shortAnswer:'Jesus promised that the gates of Hades would not overcome his church and that he would be with his people always. History shows Christians faithfully preaching the same gospel in every century.',overview:['The Latter-day Saint faith rests on the claim of a "Great Apostasy": that after the apostles died, the true church and its priesthood authority were lost, requiring a restoration through Joseph Smith in 1830.','If no total apostasy occurred, no restoration was needed.'],points:[{heading:'Jesus\' promises',body:'Jesus said, "I will build my church, and the gates of Hades will not overcome it" (Matthew 16:18), and "I am with you always, to the very end of the age" (Matthew 28:20). A complete apostasy would mean those promises failed.'},{heading:'Glory throughout all generations',body:'Paul prays for glory to God "in the church and in Christ Jesus throughout all generations" (Ephesians 3:21), which assumes the church continues in every generation.'},{heading:'The faith was delivered once',body:'Jude urges believers to contend for the faith "that was once for all entrusted to God\'s holy people" (Jude 3). It needed defending, not replacing.'},{heading:'History shows continuity',body:'Writers in every century after the apostles, such as Ignatius, Irenaeus, Athanasius and Augustine, preached the same core gospel: one God, the deity of Christ, his death and bodily resurrection, and salvation by grace.'}],objections:[{objection:'The Bible predicts a falling away (2 Thessalonians 2:3).',response:'Paul speaks of a rebellion before the return of Christ, not the disappearance of the whole church for 1,700 years. Scripture warns that false teachers would arise (Acts 20:29-30), but never that the true church would vanish.'}],verses:[{ref:'Matthew 16:18',quote:!0},{ref:'Ephesians 3:21',quote:!0},{ref:'Jude 1:3',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 28:20'}],searchTerms:'Great Apostasy Mormon restoration',keyPassage:'Matthew 16',furtherReading:['Mormonism 101 by Bill McKeever and Eric Johnson','Mormonism resources at carm.org']},{id:'lds-grace-and-works',category:'lds',title:'Grace "After All We Can Do"?',question:'How is the Latter-day Saint view of salvation different from the biblical gospel?',shortAnswer:'Latter-day Saint teaching combines grace with ongoing obedience and temple ordinances required for exaltation. The Bible teaches that we are saved by grace through faith alone, as a gift, so we can know we have eternal life.',overview:['The Book of Mormon says "it is by grace that we are saved, after all we can do" (2 Nephi 25:23), and Moroni 10:32 says grace is sufficient if believers "deny yourselves of all ungodliness." In recent years many Latter-day Saint leaders have emphasized grace more strongly.','Still, the highest level of heaven, exaltation, requires baptism and temple ordinances, keeping covenants and enduring to the end. Many members feel they can never be sure they have done enough.'],points:[{heading:'Not by works',body:'Salvation is "by grace\u2026 through faith\u2026 not by works, so that no one can boast" (Ephesians 2:8-9). God saved us "not because of righteous things we had done, but because of his mercy" (Titus 3:5).'},{heading:'Faith credited as righteousness',body:'God "justifies the ungodly" who trust him, without first requiring them to become worthy (Romans 4:5). Good works follow as the fruit of salvation, not its condition.'},{heading:'Assurance is possible',body:'John wrote "so that you may know that you have eternal life" (1 John 5:13). If salvation depends on our performance, assurance is impossible; if it rests on Christ\'s finished work, it is certain.'},{heading:'Good news for the weary',body:'Jesus invites those who are "weary and burdened" to come to him for rest (Matthew 11:28). For many Latter-day Saints, discovering that Christ has done it all is a profound relief.'}],objections:[{objection:'James says faith without works is dead.',response:'James is addressing faith that is only talk. Real saving faith produces good works as its evidence (James 2:18). Paul and James agree: we are saved by faith alone, but the faith that saves is never alone.'}],verses:[{ref:'Ephesians 2:8-9',quote:!0},{ref:'Titus 3:5',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 4:5',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 11:28',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'Mormon grace after all we can do salvation',keyPassage:'Ephesians 2',furtherReading:['Mormonism 101 by Bill McKeever and Eric Johnson','The Cross of Christ by John Stott']},{id:'lds-joseph-smith',category:'lds',title:'Was Joseph Smith a Prophet?',question:'How can we test Joseph Smith\'s claim to be a prophet of God?',shortAnswer:'The Bible gives clear tests for a prophet: his predictions must come true and his teaching must agree with what God has already revealed. Joseph Smith\'s record raises serious problems on both counts.',overview:['Latter-day Saints honor Joseph Smith as the prophet who restored the true church. Testing his claims is not an attack on Latter-day Saints but what Scripture asks of every claimed prophet, including Christian ones.','The church\'s own Gospel Topics essays acknowledge several of the issues below.'],points:[{heading:'Prophecies must come true',body:'Deuteronomy 18:22 says a prophet whose prediction fails has spoken presumptuously. In Doctrine and Covenants 84 (1832), Smith prophesied that a temple would be built in Independence, Missouri, "in this generation." It was not built.'},{heading:'Teaching must agree with Scripture',body:'Smith\'s later teachings, that God was once a man and that humans can become gods, contradict the Bible\'s clear teaching about the one eternal God (Isaiah 43:10). Even an angel preaching another gospel is to be rejected (Galatians 1:8).'},{heading:'The First Vision accounts',body:'Smith gave several accounts of his first vision. The earliest (1832) mentions only the Lord; the official 1838 account describes both the Father and the Son. The church\'s "First Vision Accounts" essay acknowledges the differences.'},{heading:'The Book of Abraham',body:'Smith said he translated the Book of Abraham from Egyptian papyri. When surviving fragments were rediscovered in 1966, Egyptologists identified them as common funerary texts. The church\'s essay on the Book of Abraham acknowledges that the fragments do not contain its text.'}],objections:[{objection:'Biblical prophets made mistakes too.',response:'Prophets were sinful people, but when they spoke "thus says the Lord," their words came true. The test in Deuteronomy 18 applies specifically to what a prophet claims God has said.'},{objection:'God has always spoken through prophets, so why not today?',response:'Hebrews 1:1-2 says God spoke through the prophets in the past, "but in these last days he has spoken to us by his Son." Jesus is God\'s final and complete word, and any later message must agree with him.'}],verses:[{ref:'Deuteronomy 18:22',quote:!0},{ref:'Hebrews 1:1-2',quote:!0},{ref:'Galatians 1:8',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'Joseph Smith false prophet test',keyPassage:'Deuteronomy 18',furtherReading:['The Mormon Mirage by Latayne Scott','Mormonism 101 by Bill McKeever and Eric Johnson','The church\'s Gospel Topics essays at churchofjesuschrist.org']}]},862,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"jesusTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const s=[{id:'historical-jesus',category:'jesus',title:'Did Jesus Really Exist?',question:'Isn\'t Jesus just a myth?',shortAnswer:'Virtually all historians, whether Christian, Jewish, agnostic or atheist, agree that Jesus of Nazareth was a real first-century Jewish teacher who was crucified under Pontius Pilate.',overview:['The "Jesus myth" theory is popular online but has almost no support among scholars of the ancient world. Even skeptical New Testament scholar Bart Ehrman wrote a book (Did Jesus Exist?) defending Jesus\' historical existence.','The evidence includes early Christian writings and several non-Christian sources written within about a century of his life.'],points:[{heading:'Early Christian sources',body:'Paul\'s letters, written within about twenty to thirty years of the crucifixion, refer to Jesus\' birth as a Jew, his brother James, his last supper, his crucifixion and burial. Paul personally met Peter and James (Galatians 1:18-19).'},{heading:'Tacitus',body:'The Roman historian Tacitus (around AD 116, Annals 15.44) writes that "Christus," from whom Christians took their name, suffered the extreme penalty under Pontius Pilate during the reign of Tiberius.'},{heading:'Josephus',body:'The Jewish historian Josephus (around AD 93) mentions "James, the brother of Jesus who was called Christ" (Antiquities 20.9.1). A longer passage (18.3.3) was likely embellished by later copyists, but most scholars believe it contains an authentic core referring to Jesus.'},{heading:'Other sources',body:'Pliny the Younger (around AD 112) describes Christians singing hymns "to Christ as to a god." The Babylonian Talmud refers to Yeshu being executed on the eve of Passover. Hostile sources confirm the basic outline.'}],objections:[{objection:'There are no contemporary eyewitness records of Jesus.',response:'Few ancient figures have records from their own lifetimes, especially rural teachers. The Gospels themselves are rooted in eyewitness testimony and were written within the lifetimes of those who knew him, which is excellent by ancient standards.'},{objection:'The Christian sources are biased.',response:'Having a viewpoint doesn\'t make a source unreliable; historians evaluate all sources critically. Many of the earliest Christians were in a position to know the facts and suffered for testifying to them.'}],verses:[{ref:'Luke 1:1-4'},{ref:'Galatians 1:18-19'},{ref:'2 Peter 1:16',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'historical evidence Jesus existed Tacitus Josephus',keyPassage:'Luke 1',furtherReading:['Did Jesus Exist? by Bart Ehrman (a skeptic\'s defense)','The Historical Jesus by Gary Habermas','Jesus and the Eyewitnesses by Richard Bauckham']},{id:'resurrection',category:'jesus',title:'The Resurrection',question:'Is there real evidence that Jesus rose from the dead?',shortAnswer:'A handful of facts accepted by most historians, including the crucifixion, the empty tomb, the appearances and the sudden rise of Christianity, are best explained by Jesus actually rising from the dead.',overview:['The resurrection is the hinge of Christianity. Paul says that if Christ has not been raised, faith is useless (1 Corinthians 15:14-17). That makes the resurrection testable in principle.','Scholars like Gary Habermas and Michael Licona use a "minimal facts" approach: they build only on facts accepted by the large majority of critical scholars, including skeptics.'],points:[{heading:'Fact 1: Jesus died by crucifixion',body:'This is attested by the Gospels, Paul, Tacitus and Josephus. Roman executioners were professionals, and even skeptical scholars treat the crucifixion as one of the most certain facts about Jesus.'},{heading:'Fact 2: The disciples believed he appeared to them',body:'In 1 Corinthians 15:3-7 Paul passes on a creed that most scholars date to within a few years of the crucifixion. It lists appearances to Peter, the Twelve, more than 500 people at once, James and all the apostles.'},{heading:'Fact 3: Skeptics were converted',body:'Paul, a persecutor of the church, and James, Jesus\' brother who did not believe during Jesus\' ministry (John 7:5), both became leaders in the church and attributed this to seeing the risen Jesus.'},{heading:'Fact 4: The tomb was empty',body:'The resurrection was proclaimed in Jerusalem, where the tomb could have been checked. The earliest Jewish counter-claim was that the disciples stole the body (Matthew 28:11-15), which concedes the tomb was empty. Women, whose testimony was undervalued at the time, are named as the first witnesses, an unlikely detail to invent.'},{heading:'Fact 5: The disciples were transformed',body:'Frightened followers became bold proclaimers willing to suffer and die. People will die for what they believe is true, but not for what they know is a lie.'}],objections:[{objection:'The disciples hallucinated.',response:'Hallucinations are individual experiences, like dreams; groups do not share the same hallucination. They also wouldn\'t explain the empty tomb or the conversion of skeptics like Paul and James.'},{objection:'Jesus didn\'t really die; he just fainted (swoon theory).',response:'Roman crucifixion was brutal and final. A half-dead man who had been scourged and crucified would not have inspired his followers to believe he had conquered death.'},{objection:'The story is a legend that grew over time.',response:'The 1 Corinthians 15 creed is too early for legendary development; it reaches back to within a few years of the events and to people Paul personally knew.'}],verses:[{ref:'1 Corinthians 15:3-8',quote:!0},{ref:'1 Corinthians 15:14',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 1:3'},{ref:'Matthew 28:11-15'}],searchTerms:'evidence for the resurrection of Jesus',keyPassage:'1 Corinthians 15',furtherReading:['The Case for the Resurrection of Jesus by Gary Habermas and Michael Licona','The Resurrection of the Son of God by N.T. Wright','The Case for Christ by Lee Strobel']},{id:'deity-of-christ',category:'jesus',title:'Is Jesus God?',question:'Did Jesus ever actually claim to be God?',shortAnswer:'Yes, both in direct statements and in actions only God could rightly do, such as forgiving sins and accepting worship. His earliest Jewish followers worshiped him as Lord alongside the Father.',overview:['Some say Jesus was only a good teacher and that later Christians turned him into God. The evidence shows that the belief in his deity goes back to Jesus himself and to the earliest church.','C.S. Lewis famously argued that Jesus\' claims leave us with limited options: he was either lying, deluded, or truly Lord.'],points:[{heading:'He did what only God can do',body:'Jesus forgave sins against others (Mark 2:5-7), prompting the scribes to ask, "Who can forgive sins but God alone?" He claimed authority to judge the world and to give eternal life.'},{heading:'He used God\'s name',body:'In John 8:58 Jesus said, "before Abraham was born, I am!" echoing God\'s self-revelation in Exodus 3:14. His listeners understood the claim and picked up stones to kill him for blasphemy.'},{heading:'He claimed the divine throne',body:'At his trial Jesus identified himself as the Son of Man of Daniel 7 who would sit at the right hand of the Mighty One and come with the clouds (Mark 14:61-64). The high priest tore his clothes and called it blasphemy.'},{heading:'He accepted worship',body:'Thomas addressed the risen Jesus as "My Lord and my God!" (John 20:28), and Jesus did not correct him, even though faithful Jews worshiped God alone.'},{heading:'The earliest church worshiped him',body:'Within twenty years, Paul could quote a hymn describing Christ as "being in very nature God" (Philippians 2:6-11) and adapt the Jewish Shema to include Jesus (1 Corinthians 8:6).'}],objections:[{objection:'Jesus never said the exact words "I am God."',response:'He made the claim in ways that were even clearer in his first-century Jewish context, by taking God\'s name, God\'s prerogatives and God\'s worship. His opponents certainly understood him.'},{objection:'Jesus prayed to the Father and said the Father is greater, so he can\'t be God.',response:'Christians believe the Son took on human nature and a servant\'s role (Philippians 2:7). Within the Trinity, the Son relates to the Father as Son, while sharing the same divine nature. See the topic on the Trinity.'}],verses:[{ref:'John 1:1',quote:!0},{ref:'John 8:58',quote:!0},{ref:'John 20:28'},{ref:'Philippians 2:5-11'},{ref:'Titus 2:13'},{ref:'Colossians 2:9'}],searchTerms:'did Jesus claim to be God deity of Christ',keyPassage:'John 8',furtherReading:['Putting Jesus in His Place by Robert Bowman and J. Ed Komoszewski','Mere Christianity by C.S. Lewis']},{id:'copycat',category:'jesus',title:'Was Jesus Copied from Pagan Myths?',question:'Isn\'t the Jesus story borrowed from Horus, Mithras or other gods?',shortAnswer:'No. These claims circulate widely online, but they rely on exaggerated or invented parallels, and specialists in ancient religion overwhelmingly reject them.',overview:['Internet memes claim that Horus, Mithras, Dionysus and others were born of virgins on December 25, had twelve disciples, were crucified and rose after three days. When checked against the primary sources, these parallels collapse.','The Gospels are firmly rooted in first-century Judaism, a culture strongly opposed to pagan religion.'],points:[{heading:'Check the sources',body:'Egyptian texts do not describe Horus as born of a virgin, having twelve disciples, or being crucified. Mithras was said to be born from a rock. Many claimed parallels come from modern authors, not ancient texts.'},{heading:'Timing problems',body:'Much of what we know about the Roman cult of Mithras comes from after the New Testament was written. If there was borrowing, it may have gone the other way.'},{heading:'Dying and rising is not resurrection',body:'Myths about gods tied to seasonal cycles of vegetation are very different from the claim that a specific man, executed under a named Roman governor, bodily rose from the grave in history.'},{heading:'Jewish context',body:'The first Christians were monotheistic Jews who despised pagan idolatry. It is historically implausible that they would build their faith on pagan myths and then die for it.'}],objections:[{objection:'But there are some similarities between religions.',response:'General similarities, such as a hero or a sacrifice, are expected across human cultures. Similarity is not borrowing. The question is whether the Gospel accounts depend on these myths, and the evidence says no.'}],verses:[{ref:'2 Peter 1:16',quote:!0},{ref:'Luke 3:1-2'}],searchTerms:'Jesus pagan myths Horus Mithras copycat',keyPassage:'2 Peter 1',furtherReading:['Reinventing Jesus by Komoszewski, Sawyer and Wallace','The Case for the Real Jesus by Lee Strobel']}]},863,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"toughTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'problem-of-evil',category:'tough',title:'The Problem of Evil',question:'If God is good and all-powerful, why is there so much evil and suffering?',shortAnswer:'God\'s goodness and power do not require him to prevent all suffering if he has good reasons for allowing it, including the gift of real freedom. And in Jesus, God has entered our suffering and promised to end it.',overview:['This is perhaps the most serious objection to faith, both intellectually and emotionally. It deserves careful thought and genuine compassion.','It helps to separate two sides: the philosophical problem (is God\'s existence logically consistent with evil?) and the personal problem (how do I trust God in my pain?).'],points:[{heading:'Love requires real freedom',body:'God made creatures capable of love, which requires the ability to choose. Freedom that could never be misused would not be real freedom. Much of the world\'s evil flows from human choices. The philosopher Alvin Plantinga showed that there is no logical contradiction between God and evil.'},{heading:'We aren\'t in a position to judge',body:'If an infinitely wise God exists, we would expect that many of his reasons are beyond our view. Joseph could say of his brothers\' betrayal, "You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good" (Genesis 50:20), yet only years later.'},{heading:'Evil points to a standard',body:'Calling anything truly evil assumes an objective standard of good. Without God it becomes hard to say that evil really exists, only that some things are unpleasant to us. The problem of evil is also a problem for atheism.'},{heading:'God is not distant from suffering',body:'Christianity uniquely claims that God entered the world and suffered alongside us. At the cross, Jesus bore injustice, pain and death. Whatever his reasons for allowing evil, it is not because he doesn\'t care.'},{heading:'Suffering is not the end of the story',body:'God promises to wipe away every tear and make all things new (Revelation 21:4). Eternity will put present suffering in a new light (Romans 8:18).'}],objections:[{objection:'Free will doesn\'t explain earthquakes and disease.',response:'Scripture teaches that creation itself was affected by human rebellion (Romans 8:20-22). A stable world with consistent natural laws, necessary for meaningful action, also allows for natural disasters. Christians admit this is hard, while trusting God\'s character revealed in Christ.'},{objection:'If I were God, I would stop it.',response:'God has promised to stop it, fully and finally. His patience in delaying judgment is so that more people may come to repentance (2 Peter 3:9). Stopping all evil today would mean stopping all of us.'}],verses:[{ref:'Genesis 50:20',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 8:28',quote:!0},{ref:'Revelation 21:4',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 8:18-22'},{ref:'Job 38-42'}],searchTerms:'problem of evil why does God allow suffering',keyPassage:'Romans 8',furtherReading:['The Problem of Pain by C.S. Lewis','Walking with God through Pain and Suffering by Timothy Keller','God, Freedom, and Evil by Alvin Plantinga']},{id:'hell',category:'tough',title:'How Can a Loving God Send People to Hell?',question:'Isn\'t eternal punishment unfair and unloving?',shortAnswer:'A good judge cannot ignore evil. Hell is the just result of rejecting God, who is the source of all good, and God has done everything necessary, even dying on a cross, so that no one has to go there.',overview:['Hell is one of the hardest teachings of Scripture, and Jesus spoke about it more than anyone else in the Bible, always with gravity and warning, never with glee.','Understanding hell requires understanding both God\'s justice and his love, which meet at the cross.'],points:[{heading:'Love and justice go together',body:'We rightly admire judges who punish wrongdoing, and we would call a judge corrupt who let a murderer walk free. A God who never judged evil would not be loving; he would be indifferent to victims.'},{heading:'God respects human choice',body:'C.S. Lewis wrote that the doors of hell are "locked on the inside." In the end, God gives people what they have chosen: life apart from him. Since God is the source of all light, love and joy, separation from him is devastating.'},{heading:'God desires everyone to be saved',body:'God takes no pleasure in the death of the wicked (Ezekiel 33:11) and is patient, not wishing that any should perish (2 Peter 3:9). He sent his Son so that whoever believes should not perish.'},{heading:'The cross shows the cost',body:'Christ bore the judgment our sin deserves (Romans 5:8). Hell is not the default God wants for anyone; it is the reality he rescued us from at great cost to himself.'}],objections:[{objection:'Why should finite sins deserve infinite punishment?',response:'The seriousness of a wrong depends partly on whom it is against. Sin is rebellion against an infinitely holy God. Also, those in hell may continue to reject God, so the separation continues.'},{objection:'What about people who never heard of Jesus?',response:'God judges fairly according to the light each person has received (Romans 1-2), and he knows every heart. Scripture also shows God actively drawing seekers to himself. We can trust the Judge of all the earth to do right (Genesis 18:25).'}],verses:[{ref:'John 3:16',quote:!0},{ref:'2 Peter 3:9',quote:!0},{ref:'Romans 5:8',quote:!0},{ref:'Ezekiel 33:11'},{ref:'Genesis 18:25'}],searchTerms:'how can a loving God send people to hell',keyPassage:'John 3',furtherReading:['The Great Divorce by C.S. Lewis','The Reason for God by Timothy Keller (chapter 5)']},{id:'exclusivity',category:'tough',title:'Is Jesus the Only Way?',question:'Isn\'t it narrow-minded to say only one religion is true?',shortAnswer:'Every truth claim excludes its opposite, including the claim that "all religions are true." Christianity\'s claim rests on who Jesus is and what he did, and its door is open to everyone.',overview:['Many people feel that saying only one path leads to God is arrogant. But the question is not whether a claim is comfortable; it is whether it is true.','The world\'s religions make contradictory claims about God, Jesus and salvation. They cannot all be true.'],points:[{heading:'Religions really do differ',body:'Christianity says Jesus died and rose again; Islam says he was not crucified. Buddhism is non-theistic in its classic forms; Hinduism includes many gods; Judaism and Islam insist on one. Saying they all teach the same thing disrespects what they actually teach.'},{heading:'Pluralism is also exclusive',body:'The claim "all religions lead to God" excludes the views of every religion that disagrees. Everyone holds some exclusive truth claims; the question is which are true.'},{heading:'Jesus is uniquely qualified',body:'Other teachers claimed to show the way; Jesus claimed to be the way (John 14:6). He backed that claim by rising from the dead. If he did, his words carry unique authority.'},{heading:'An inclusive invitation',body:'Christianity is exclusive in its claim but inclusive in its offer. Salvation is a free gift available to anyone from any culture, not earned by religious performance.'}],objections:[{objection:'It\'s like the blind men and the elephant: each religion sees part of the truth.',response:'In that parable, the storyteller sees the whole elephant and knows every blind man is partly wrong. The parable assumes someone has the full view, the very thing it says no one has.'}],verses:[{ref:'John 14:6',quote:!0},{ref:'Acts 4:12',quote:!0},{ref:'Revelation 7:9'},{ref:'1 Timothy 2:5-6'}],searchTerms:'is Jesus the only way to God exclusivity',keyPassage:'John 14',furtherReading:['The Reason for God by Timothy Keller','Jesus Among Other Gods by Ravi Zacharias']},{id:'science-and-faith',category:'tough',title:'Science and Faith',question:'Hasn\'t science disproved God?',shortAnswer:'No. Modern science was largely born in a Christian worldview that expected an orderly, understandable creation, and many leading scientists past and present are believers. Science explains how nature works; it does not explain away the One who made it.',overview:['The idea that science and religion are at war was popularized in the nineteenth century, but historians of science now widely reject this "conflict thesis" as a myth.','Science and theology answer different kinds of questions, and where they overlap, such as the beginning of the universe, they often point in the same direction.'],points:[{heading:'Christian roots of science',body:'Pioneers such as Kepler, Boyle, Newton, Faraday and Maxwell believed that studying nature was "thinking God\'s thoughts after him." They expected order because they believed in a rational Lawgiver.'},{heading:'Believers in science today',body:'Georges Lemaitre, a Catholic priest, first proposed what became the Big Bang theory. Francis Collins, who led the Human Genome Project, is an outspoken Christian. Faith and rigorous science coexist in many lives.'},{heading:'Mechanism is not agency',body:'Explaining how a car engine works does not disprove the existence of Henry Ford. Knowing how nature operates does not tell us whether a Mind stands behind it.'},{heading:'Science presupposes things it can\'t prove',body:'Science assumes that nature is orderly, that our minds can grasp it, and that logic and mathematics apply to it. Theism explains these assumptions naturally.'}],objections:[{objection:'What about Galileo?',response:'The Galileo affair involved politics, personalities and a scientific debate that was not yet settled, not simply faith against science. Galileo himself remained a believer and argued that Scripture and nature, rightly understood, cannot conflict.'},{objection:'Evolution makes God unnecessary.',response:'Christians hold a range of views on the age of the earth and on evolution. But no biological theory can explain why there is a universe at all, why its laws are fine-tuned, or why it is intelligible.'}],verses:[{ref:'Psalm 19:1',quote:!0},{ref:'Proverbs 25:2'},{ref:'Romans 1:20',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'science and Christianity conflict',keyPassage:'Psalm 104',furtherReading:['God\'s Undertaker by John Lennox','The Language of God by Francis Collins','Where the Conflict Really Lies by Alvin Plantinga']},{id:'ot-violence',category:'tough',title:'Violence in the Old Testament',question:'How can the God of the Old Testament command war against the Canaanites?',shortAnswer:'These were limited, specific acts of judgment on deeply corrupt cultures after centuries of patience, often described with the exaggerated war rhetoric typical of the ancient Near East. They are not an ongoing command for anyone today.',overview:['These passages are genuinely troubling, and Christians should not dismiss the question. Reading them carefully in their historical and literary context changes how they look.','Scholars like Paul Copan and Matthew Flannagan have written extensively on this topic.'],points:[{heading:'God waited for centuries',body:'God told Abraham that his descendants would wait four generations because "the iniquity of the Amorite is not yet full" (Genesis 15:16). The judgment came after a long period of patience and repeated warning.'},{heading:'Judgment on real evil',body:'Canaanite religion included practices such as child sacrifice (Leviticus 18:21, Deuteronomy 12:31). Israel was warned that it would face the same judgment if it did the same things, and later it did.'},{heading:'Ancient war rhetoric',body:'Ancient Near Eastern military accounts routinely used sweeping language like "utterly destroyed" even when many people survived. The Bible itself shows that the Canaanites remained afterward (for example Judges 1), suggesting hyperbole about driving out, not literal extermination.'},{heading:'Unique and unrepeatable',body:'These commands applied to a specific moment in redemptive history. They offer no license for violence today, and Jesus explicitly rejected using the sword to advance his kingdom (Matthew 26:52, John 18:36).'}],objections:[{objection:'The God of the Old Testament is different from Jesus.',response:'The Old Testament repeatedly describes God as "compassionate and gracious God, slow to anger, abounding in love and faithfulness" (Exodus 34:6), and Jesus spoke of judgment more than anyone. The same God shows both justice and mercy in both testaments, and they meet at the cross.'}],verses:[{ref:'Genesis 15:16'},{ref:'Deuteronomy 9:4-5'},{ref:'Exodus 34:6-7'},{ref:'Ezekiel 18:23'}],searchTerms:'Canaanite conquest Old Testament violence',keyPassage:'Deuteronomy 9',furtherReading:['Is God a Moral Monster? by Paul Copan','Did God Really Command Genocide? by Paul Copan and Matthew Flannagan']}]},864,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"worldviewTopics",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'trinity',category:'worldviews',title:'The Trinity',question:'How can God be three and one at the same time? Isn\'t that a contradiction?',shortAnswer:'The Trinity is not "one God and three Gods" or "one person and three persons." It is one God (one "what") who eternally exists as three persons (three "whos"): Father, Son and Holy Spirit.',overview:['The word "Trinity" is not in the Bible, but the teaching is. Christians summarized what Scripture says in three statements: there is only one God; the Father, Son and Spirit are each fully God; and the three are distinct persons.','Understanding the Trinity is essential when talking with Muslims, Jehovah\'s Witnesses, Oneness Pentecostals and others who reject it.'],points:[{heading:'There is one God',body:'Scripture is emphatically monotheistic: "Hear, O Israel: The LORD our God, the LORD is one" (Deuteronomy 6:4). Jesus affirmed this (Mark 12:29).'},{heading:'Three persons are called God',body:'The Father is God (John 6:27). The Son is God (John 1:1, 20:28; Titus 2:13). The Holy Spirit is God; lying to him is lying to God (Acts 5:3-4).'},{heading:'The three are distinct',body:'At Jesus\' baptism the Son is baptized, the Spirit descends and the Father speaks from heaven (Matthew 3:16-17). The Son prays to the Father and sends the Spirit. They relate to each other personally.'},{heading:'One name',body:'Jesus commanded baptism "in the name (singular) of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit" (Matthew 28:19).'},{heading:'Why it matters',body:'If God is Trinity, love is eternal: the Father, Son and Spirit have loved each other forever. God did not need to create in order to love. The Trinity also makes the gospel possible, since the Father sends the Son, who is himself God, to save us.'}],objections:[{objection:'1 + 1 + 1 = 3, not 1.',response:'The doctrine does not claim three persons are one person, which would be a contradiction. It says God is one in being and three in person. Different categories are being counted.'},{objection:'The Trinity was invented at the Council of Nicaea.',response:'Nicaea (AD 325) clarified language to defend what churches already believed against Arius. Earlier writers such as Ignatius (around AD 110) called Jesus God, and the New Testament itself uses triadic formulas (2 Corinthians 13:14).'}],verses:[{ref:'Deuteronomy 6:4',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 28:19',quote:!0},{ref:'Matthew 3:16-17'},{ref:'2 Corinthians 13:14'},{ref:'Acts 5:3-4'}],searchTerms:'doctrine of the Trinity explained',keyPassage:'John 1',furtherReading:['The Forgotten Trinity by James White','Delighting in the Trinity by Michael Reeves']},{id:'relativism',category:'worldviews',title:'Is Truth Relative?',question:'"That may be true for you, but not for me." Isn\'t all truth relative?',shortAnswer:'The statement "all truth is relative" presents itself as absolutely true, so it refutes itself. Truth is what corresponds to reality, and reality doesn\'t change based on our preferences.',overview:['Relativism is common in Western culture, especially about religion and morality. Often it is motivated by a good desire to be humble and tolerant.','But true tolerance requires disagreement: you only tolerate views you think are mistaken. And humility is best served by pursuing truth together, not denying it exists.'],points:[{heading:'Relativism is self-defeating',body:'If "there is no absolute truth" is absolutely true, then it is false. If it is only relatively true, there is no reason anyone else should accept it.'},{heading:'Preferences vs. facts',body:'Your favorite ice cream is true "for you." Whether Jesus rose from the dead is not that kind of claim. It either happened or it didn\'t, regardless of what anyone believes.'},{heading:'Nobody lives as a relativist',body:'Relativists still object when they are lied to, robbed or treated unjustly. Our reactions to wrongs done to us reveal that we believe in real moral truth.'},{heading:'Jesus claimed to be the truth',body:'Jesus told Pilate he came to testify to the truth, and Pilate replied, "What is truth?" (John 18:37-38). Jesus claimed not just to teach truth but to be the truth (John 14:6).'}],objections:[{objection:'Different cultures have different morals, so morality is relative.',response:'Disagreement doesn\'t mean there is no right answer; cultures have disagreed about whether the earth is round. And beneath the surface, cultures share remarkably similar core moral values, as C.S. Lewis documented in The Abolition of Man.'}],verses:[{ref:'John 18:37-38'},{ref:'John 8:32',quote:!0},{ref:'John 14:6',quote:!0}],searchTerms:'moral relativism absolute truth',keyPassage:'John 18',furtherReading:['Relativism: Feet Firmly Planted in Mid-Air by Francis Beckwith and Greg Koukl','The Abolition of Man by C.S. Lewis']}]},865,[]);
